    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/3341827/lecturer-field-supervisor-uhm-college-of-education-institute-for-teacher-educat": {
      "title": "Lecturer; Field Supervisor (UHM/College of Education/Institute for Teacher Education-Secondary)",
      "description": "Lecturer; Field Supervisor (UHM/College of Education/Institute for Teacher Education-Secondary) Salary Depends on Qualifications Location UH Manoa - College of Ed, Various Locations, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2021-01327 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education Opening Date 12/09/2021 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: Pending Description Title: Lecturer; Field Supervisor (UHM/College of Education/Institute for Teacher Education – Secondary) Hiring Unit: College of Education Location: Manoa Date Posted: December 9, 2021 Closing Date: Continuous Salary: Minimum of $1,671 per credit hour Other conditions: Lecturer applicant pool for future semesters; temporary, part-time work, as needed, contingent upon student enrollment, position availability and funding. May be for day, evening, or weekend offerings on- and off-campus. Selectee is required to complete the COE New Faculty orientation in the first year and pass a criminal background check and Title IX training. Duties and Responsibilities Coordinate field placements with principals and teachers. Supervise, mentor and evaluate students in field-based settings in secondary classrooms. Conduct field experience seminars and student teaching seminars. Evaluate candidates' lesson plans and field-based program assignments. Conduct midterm and end-of-semester conferences. Participate in collaborative relationships with mentor teachers, school administrators, and staff. Provide service to the department, university and broader community. Minimum Qualifications Earned master's degree in Secondary Education, or equivalent combination of graduate education and experience. Working knowledge of national content standards, Hawaii Content and Performance Standards III, InTASC Standards, and Common Core Standards. Three years full-time K-12 classroom teaching experience, as teacher of record. Experience working with various populations. Desirable Qualifications Successful post-secondary teaching experience. Experience working with native Hawaiian populations. Experience teaching on-line courses. Experience with school/university partnerships. Experience mentoring students in field settings. Experience in field-based, standards-based teacher education. Experience working with special education students and/or multilingual learners. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Documents include: (1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) name and contact information of three (3) professional references and (4) official transcripts (copies of official transcripts are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required at the time of hire.) Incomplete applications will not be considered. The application will be considered incomplete if material is unreadable. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Institute for Teacher Education Secondary Program; itesec@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5436747/lecturers-csd-spapp-courses": {
      "title": "Lecturers (CSD SPAPP Courses)",
      "description": "Lecturers (CSD SPAPP Courses) Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Kaka'ako - Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01710 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Communication Sciences and Disorders Opening Date 08/04/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: Lecturer Description Title: Lecturers (CSD SPAPP Courses) Position Number: Pending Hiring Unit: UHM School of Medicine - Department of Communication Sciences & Disorders Location: JABSOM Kaka’ako – Goldbond Building Date Posted: August 4, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment, first review August 19, 2026 Salary Information: Minimum LA $2,053 per credit hour Full Time/Part Time: Part Time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Fall, Spring and Summer semesters, part-time, for lecturer candidate pool, non-tenurial, assignments as needed, contingent on availability of funds, satisfactory performance, and actual staffing requirements. Duties and Responsibilities: Teach various undergraduate online or graduate level courses in the Dept. of Communication Sciences and Disorders (CSD) including and not limited to: Undergraduate Online Courses in Fall, Spring, Summer: CSD432 – Phonetics (Fall) CSD436 – Intro to Comm Sci & Disorders (Fall) CSD433 – Speech and Lang Development (Spring) CSD435 – Intro to Clinical Methods in CSD (Spring) CSD431 – Anatomy and Physiology (Summer) CSD434 – Acoustics and Phychoacoustics (Summer) CSD437 – Intro to Audiometry and Auditory Disorders (Summer) CSD438 – Statistics Maintain at least one office hour per week for each class taught. Provide instruction and guidance to meet student learning objectives, prepare course syllabus and reports related to teaching and student progress. Work under supervision of discipline coordinator/division chairperson in integrating teaching efforts with other instructors in the discipline area. Student and peer evaluations and self-assessment of teaching effectiveness are required. Other scholarly, clinical, and administrative duties as assigned. Lecturer A Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in Communication Sciences and Disorders or related field from college or university of recognized standing, with major work in CSD. Demonstrated ability as a teacher. Poise and good address for meeting, collaborating, and conferring with others. Lecturer B Minimum Qualifications: Same as Lecturer A, with addition of the following: Terminal doctoral degree (i.e. PhD or EdD) in Communication Sciences and Disorders or related field from an accredited college or university of recognized standing. Doctor of Audiology degree from an accredited program in a college or university of recognized standing. Demonstrated record of teaching at the undergraduate level. Evidence of scholarly or professional activity. Lecturer C Minimum Qualifications: Same as Lecturer B, with addition of the following: Demonstrated record of substantial teaching experience at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Extensive scholarly or professional activity. Desirable Qualifications: Experience teaching online courses. Experience in working in a multicultural setting. To Apply: Submit original cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, CV, copies of licenses and certifications, names and contact information of 5 professional references (including email addresses) and official transcripts (or original documentation from the institution confirming degree). Copies of transcripts are accepted; however, original transcripts are required upon hire. Please note that incomplete applications will not be considered. Address: Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders University of Hawaii at Manoa, John A. Burns School of Medicine 677 Ala Moana Blvd., Suite 625 Honolulu, HI 96813 Inquiries: Keith Yamase; 808-692-1583; keithy@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/2382838/lecturers-school-of-architecture": {
      "title": "Lecturers (School of Architecture)",
      "description": "Lecturers (School of Architecture) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2019-00363 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Architecture Opening Date 03/12/2019 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: Pending Description Lecturers for the UH Manoa School of Architecture: Part-time, for placement in lecturer pool for fall 2026 and future semesters, dependent on availability of funds and actual staffing requirements. Classroom lecturers or studio instructors in design, landscape architecture, history and theory, materials and construction, professional practice and other related topics in Environmental Design and/or Architecture. Note: Placement in the pool ensures eligibility only and does not guarantee employment. Duties & Responsibilities Teach one or more courses in area of expertise as assigned by the Dean of the School of Architecture. Minimum Qualifications Professional NAAB Accredited Degree or Ph.D. or equivalent in architecture, landscape architecture or related field. Desirable Qualifications Experience teaching at the college or university level To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button in the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach all the required documents listed below in PDF format: Cover letter summarizing interest and qualifications for the position, including experience and impacts in teaching, research or creative work, and service (maximum two pages). Curriculum Vitae Statement of teaching philosophy (maximum one page) Portfolio of research and/or creative projects (maximum twenty pages) Names and contact information of three professional references Official transcripts showing degrees (copies of official transcripts are acceptable at time of application; however original official transcripts will be required upon hire) Inquiries: Charlene Lagondino at lagondin@hawaii.edu. EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5482204/maui-county-administrator-0089215-ctahr-maui": {
      "title": "Maui County Administrator (0089215, CTAHR/Maui)",
      "description": "Maui County Administrator (0089215, CTAHR/Maui) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Kahului, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-01979 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Maui County Opening Date 09/14/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0089215 Description Questions Description Title : Maui County Administrator Position Number: 0089215 Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), Maui County Location: Kahului Extension Office (CARES Kahului), Kahului, HI; Maui Island Date Posted : September 14, 2026 Closing Date : November 16, 2026, 11:59PM HST Band : SM2 Salary : Salary Schedule Full Time/Part Time : Full-time Monthly Type: 11 Month Temporary/Permanent : Permanent Other Conditions : For best consideration, all application materials should be submitted by November 16, 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Introduction Provides leadership for CTAHR Extension, Research, and Instructional programs in Maui county. Manages and maintains two CTAHR Cooperative Extension offices (Moloka`i and Kahului) and four Research stations (Kula Agriculture Park Site, Maui Agricultural Research Center, Haleakala Research Station, and Moloka`i Applied Research Farm) in Maui County. Duties and Responsibilities Assumes responsibility for and coordinates all CTAHR research, extension, and instruction activities for Maui County. In conjunction with department chairpersons, develops objectives, priorities, and plans for CTAHR research, extension, and instruction programs with emphasis upon the needs of the county. Participates with CTAHR department chairpersons and cabinet administrators in the development of statewide plans for the development of agricultural industries, commodities, and natural and human resources to assure coordination between county and State plans. Reviews and recommends funding through departments for annual county plans of work and projects prepared by faculty members in the county based on county plans and priorities. Monitors and evaluates county programs and prepares reports in accordance with Federal, State, University, and CTAHR regulations and requirements. Organizes for CTAHR county staff participation in county projects and community development and improvement activities. Exercises academic and professional leadership in planning and carrying out program activities based on professional respect and trust and principles of academic responsibility and collegiality. Exercises independent judgement in handling a wide variety of problems involving a highly diverse population of clientele groups and individuals. Responsible for the operations and maintenance of research stations and extension offices. Including repairs and maintenance of facilities and equipment and recommends CIP projects as necessary. Collaborates with the CTAHR Cabinet and other county administrators to assure uniform, statewide policies, procedures, and regulatory compliance. Hires, supervises, and evaluates county staff. Responsible for custody, allocation and maintenance of budgets, space, equipment, and facilities for all research, extension, and instruction activities in the county. Assesses all county-based faculty in the promotion and tenure process at the direction of the dean. Administers applicable fiscal and personnel policies and procedures. Represents the College in the county and represents county interests in dealings with other units in the College. Represents county interests in the planning and program activities of the College. Officially represent the College in dealings with the county government, Federal, and State agencies (including regulatory agencies) located in the county, various advisory groups, industry and community organizations and leaders, and the general public. Maintains an effective working relation with these organizations and individuals. Serves as a public service leader in the community and interacts with other leadership segments in the county. Secures support from the academic departments and other College units in support of county programs and projects. Secures resources to support county-based programs and projects form extramural sources. Markets CTAHR programs and activities. Conducts special projects of importance to the College. These projects may include scholarly work in research, instruction, or outreach. Performs other administrative duties as assigned by the Dean. List of Direct Reports As reflected in the most current organizational chart . Authority and Decision-Making Exercised Guided by University of Hawai’i and CTAHR policies and procedures. County Administrators function independently, taking into account the different situations and needs of the particular county. Exercises independent judgment in handling a wide variety of problems involving a highly diverse population of clientele groups and individuals. Direction and Review of Work Received Outcomes are checked against planned objectives with input by clientele groups, faculty, and staff. Contacts and Relationships All CTAHR and University of Maui units; Federal, State, and county agencies: industry and community organizations and leaders; individual group clienteles; general public. Must function under constant public scrutiny. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree or higher in an area related to agriculture, natural resource management or human resources. Attained the academic rank of I4, F4, R4, or A4 (associate professor, associate researcher, associate specialist, or associate extension agent); or has demonstrated a record of comparable professional experience prior to appointment. Professional administrative experience such as 4H Director, Graduate Chair, Undergraduate Chair, Department Chair, County Administrator, Assistant Dean, etc. Knowledge and experience with applied research and extension programs. Ability to plan and implement programs and projects for the benefit of Maui County; Ability to establish and maintain effective relationships with the public and volunteers. Ability to make independent judgments and decisions. Ability to provide academic, programmatic and professional leadership and mentorship. Desirable Qualifications Attained the academic rank of I5, F5, R5, A5 (full professor, researcher, specialist, or extension agent), or has demonstrated a record of comparable professional experience prior to appointment. Previous administrative experience in University or county governmental setting or equivalent. Experience in or knowledge of the county in which position is located. Proven grantsmanship ability. Strong knowledge and experience in the land-grant University system. Ability to work with diverse groups, clientele, agencies, individuals, and stakeholders. Experience in managing budgets for large programs; experience in managing facility repair and maintenance projects. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Statement of how your professional experience meets MQ #2 and #3; Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Dr. Parwinder Grewal, pgrewal@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ 01 Do you possess a Master’s degree or higher in an area related to agriculture, natural resource management or human resources? Yes No 02 Have you attained the academic rank of I4, F4, R4, or A4 (associate professor, associate researcher, associate specialist, or associate extension agent); or has demonstrated a record of comparable professional experience prior to appointment? Yes No 03 Do you have professional administrative experience such as 4H Director, Graduate Chair, Undergraduate Chair, Department Chair, County Administrator, Assistant Dean, etc? Yes No 04 Do you have knowledge and experience with applied research and extension programs? How many years: Less than 1 year 1 - 2 years 2 - 4 years 5 - 10 years 10+ years 05 Complete applications and all requested document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you attest that you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5391677/mens-basketball-team-manager": {
      "title": "Men’s Basketball Team Manager",
      "description": "Men’s Basketball Team Manager Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01398 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 06/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: Pending Description Title: Men’s Basketball Team Manager Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Location: Men’s Basketball Date Posted: June 27, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment until programmatic needs are met. Other Conditions : Position renewable on availability of funds and satisfactory performance. Continuous recruitment until programmatic needs are met. For best consideration, review of applications will begin July 7, 2026 Duties and Responsibilities: Under the supervision of the Head Men’s Basketball Coach or designee, the Student Team Manager will assist with all managerial aspects of the program including clerical work such as inputting of data and filing paperwork, preparation of practice/game facilities and equipment, preparation of practice/game uniforms and personnel equipment, clock management, stat keeping, filming during practices, and assistance with team functions. Demonstrate effective organizational skills and time management. Must demonstrate a positive and professional working attitude and maintain a collaborative working relationship with the coaching staff, team members, support staff, administrators in the UH Athletics Department and other individuals associated with the UH Men’s Basketball program. Responsible for the set up and clean up of practice / game facilities and equipment for the UH Men’s Basketball team. Set up facilities and equipment for visiting teams’ practices and shoot-arounds. Responsible for the distribution, collection, maintenance, and laundry of all practice / game uniforms and player equipment. Ensure reasonable cleanliness of Stan Sheriff Center locker room, coaches’ lounge, and program conference room. Participate in limited on court activities during practice involving student-athletes (e.g. rebounding and assisting with drills). A manager shall not provide instruction to student-athletes and shall not participate in countable athletically related activities (e.g., practice player) except as permitted by Bylaw 11.01.7-(b). Manage scoreboard, game clock, and shot clock during practice. Record statistics as assigned by the coaching staff during practice. Complete clerical office work and record keeping e.g. inputting of data and filing paperwork. Help assistant coaches with clerical aspect of scouting reports. Assist the Director of Basketball Operations with administrative duties including recruiting mailouts, camp mailouts, and camp operations. Film practices, individual workouts, and games as required. Assist the performance analyst with film exchange, and coding of practices and games. Manage team bench during games (provide water / towels to players, and perform any assigned statistical duties). Attend all team practices and home games. Be available prior, during, and after practices and competitions as assigned. Travel to away games as assigned. Attend all basketball related events including but not limited to promotional activities such as pep rallies, cafeteria and dorm promotions, and other UHM athletic events where the team is required to attend. Comply with UH Men’s Basketball Team guidelines. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Know, recognize, and comply with laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated Conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if you have reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees, students, or others have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Be accepted and enrolled as a full-time student as an undergraduate (12 credits at the University of Hawai‘i at Manoa (UHM) or graduate student (8 credits at UHM) in a degree program. Knowledge and understanding of all aspects of basketball as played at the intercollegiate level. Familiarity with Microsoft Office (Word & Excel) and Mac operating systems Ability to communicate effectively and work as part of a team Ability to work independently and use initiative to solve problems Ability to be punctual and manage time wisely. Benefits: Maximum amount a team manager may receive will be equal to an undergraduate resident tuition or 100% of the individual’s tuition cost (whichever is less). Tuition does not include payment for Outreach College extension courses. Team Manager will be responsible for payment to Outreach College. Tuition payment will be prorated if the start date and completion of all required paperwork is after the first day of school. Monthly stipend for room and board, approximate amount is $1,111 (rate may be prorated based on start date) from September 2026 to May 2027 (9 months). Start Date: To be determined. To Apply : Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete and application 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the qualifications 2) resume and 3) names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references. Inquiries: Eran Ganot, Head Men’s Basketball Coach, eran@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5479101/observatory-observing-assistant-casual": {
      "title": "Observatory Observing Assistant (CASUAL)",
      "description": "Observatory Observing Assistant (CASUAL) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - IFA, Hilo, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01959 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Institute for Astronomy Opening Date 10/22/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: CASUAL Description * * * * Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part­time employees. Title: Casual Hire - Observatory Observing Assistant Hiring Unit: Institute for Astronomy Location: Hilo, HI Date Posted: October 22, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous Salary : $23.25/hour *Updated* Full Time / Part Time: Part-time Other Conditions: Position is a temporary casual hire position and offers no benefits. C ontinuous recruitment until programmatic needs are met. Duties and Responsibilities: Accompany IRTF Telescope Operators on Mauna Kea during remote observing sessions Assist Telescope Operators with the following tasks: -Fill up instruments with liquid nitrogen -Turn on air compressor -Turn off air handling unit -Assist with Instrument changes Ensure safety of personnel at the telescope Perform all other tasks as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in any field; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices & techniques in the area of astronomy or science demonstrated by knowledge, understanding & ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge of principles, practices & techniques in the area of research or telescope or mechanical experience demonstrated by knowledge, understanding & ability to apply concepts, terminology. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes & resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of research or telescope or mechanical experience Demonstrated ability to understand oral & written documentation, write reports & procedures, & communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish & maintain effective working relationships with internal & external organizations, groups, team members & individuals Minimum of two (2) years college-level coursework and six (6) months of experience in a research setting involving the collection and/or analysis of data. Must be able to work independently. Must be able to work night shift, day shift, or a combination of both Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirement fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's License (class 3), comparable driver's license or use of means of transportation which meets position needs. Desirable Qualifications: Coursework with an emphasis on Physics and/or Astronomy First Aid/ CPR training To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, and 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number) Inquiries : Callie Matulonis, IRTF Research Associate, mcnew@hawaii.edu and Kayla Maki, HR Specialist, kymaki@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5534629/operations-coordinator-0097757": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator (0097757)",
      "description": "Operations Coordinator (0097757) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02315 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Chancellor's Office Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097757 Description Title: Operations Coordinator Position Number: 0097757 Hiring Unit: OFFICE OF THE CHANCELLOR Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: November 16, 2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Provides administrative and operational assistance to the Office of the Chancellor, supporting daily office functions and facilitating efficient administrative processes. Reports directly to the Chief Administrative Specialist to the Chancellor. 2. *Manages scheduling, logistical details, and related arrangements for campus visits, institutional meetings, and executive-level engagements. 3. *Oversees the progression of high-priority campus requests, strategic initiatives, and institutional directives from assignment to completion; initiates and enforces follow-up actions across campus units to ensure executive deadlines are met. 4. *Gathers data and prepares special, nonrecurring written reports, briefing summaries, and project tracking logs for senior management regarding office workflows and ongoing campus initiatives. 5. *Evaluates and develops improved daily office procedures, including document management workflows, routing systems for incoming correspondence, and executive meeting protocols to keep the office running smoothly. 6. *Advises the Chief Administrative Specialist to the Chancellor on project timelines, flags potential administrative delays or scheduling conflicts, and recommends practical solutions to keep tasks on track. 7. *Independently investigates and resolves routine operational and administrative matters, applying a clear understanding of University policies and executive directives to address questions from internal and external stakeholders. 8. *Provides clear guidance and administrative support to colleges, program offices, and campus personnel regarding the Chancellor's Office protocols and processing timelines. 9. Manages fiscal accountability and purchasing documentation for the unit, including p-card transactions, purchase orders, and equipment/supply inventory in accordance with University policies and regulations. 10. Supervises, trains, and schedules student assistants, setting clear performance expectations to maintain a professional, secure, and confidential environment. 11. Other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential functions Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Communications, Social Sciences, or a related field and 3 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for office management, operations coordination, or executive administrative support; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of office administration as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with office administration. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and proficiency using Microsoft Office applications (Word, Excel, Outlook, and PowerPoint) and Google Workspace applications (Docs, Sheets, Drive, Gmail, and Calendar). 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Demonstrated ability to handle highly confidential and sensitive information with discretion, integrity, and sound judgment. 10. Demonstrated ability to exercise independent judgment in determining the appropriate handling, sharing, retention, and safeguarding of confidential information. 11. Demonstrated ability to represent an executive office with professionalism, tact, diplomacy, and discretion in communications and interactions with internal and external constituents. Desirable Qualifications 1. Experience working in a higher education environment providing direct operational support within an executive or central administrative office. 2. Familiarity with the University of Hawai'i system structure, campus operations, and applicable institutional administrative rules and policies. 3. Ability to work early mornings, nights, weekends and holidays, as needed, to support Chancellor's priorities and campus events. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Applicants must submit the following: 1. Cover lette r indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; 2. Resume or CV; 3. Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of 3 professional references ; 4. Copy of unofficial transcript (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries will NOT be accepted. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted.) Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. IMPORTANT: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the Last one will be reviewed. Please DO NOT include any self-identifying photos, REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBERS AND BIRTH DATE on any documents within the application packet. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. Incomplete or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Inquiries: Michelle Isa-Atta, 808-956-8456 isam@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5495103/operations-coordinator-0077284t": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator, #0077284T",
      "description": "Operations Coordinator, #0077284T Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02031 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - William S. Richardson School of Law Division Instruction Opening Date 09/24/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0077284T Description Title: Operations Coordinator Position Number: 0077284T Hiring Unit: SCH OF LAW, INSTRUCTION, INSTRUCTION Location: UH at Manoa Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Continuous recruitment until position is filled. Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Leads the day-to-day administrative and operational activities of the Center for Ocean Policy, implementing systems, workflows, operating procedures, and organizational infrastructure. 2. *Develops administrative policies, templates, tracking systems, and standard operating procedures that improve organizational effectiveness, accountability, and continuity. 3. *Provides strategic administrative support to Co-Directors through research, analysis, executive briefings, decision-support materials, and coordination of high-priority Center initiatives. 4. *Serves as the principal administrative liaison among the Department of Oceanography, William S. Richardson School of Law, University offices, faculty, students, and external stakeholders. 5. *Coordinates administrative implementation of the center's interdisciplinary educational program, including student placements, clinics, capstones, experiential learning opportunities, field activities, and related activities. 6. *Supports recruitment, selection, and appointment of Oceanography Graduate Student Fellows and Law JD Fellows. 7. *Supports faculty-led research, outreach, policy, and community engagement activities. 8. *Oversees fiscal administration of Center budgets, University and Foundation accounts, procurement, purchasing, contracts, travel, reimbursements, and operational expenditures. 9. *Monitors and analyzes financial activity, preparing budget projections and reports, and advises the Co-Directors regarding budget status, spending trends, and projected resource needs. 10. *Coordinates acquisition and management of equipment, software, communications infrastructure, office resources, and operational support services. 11. *Coordinates planning and implementation of seminars, workshops, conferences, symposia, public engagement activities, and invited speaker programs. 12. *Develops and maintains the Center's website, communications materials, newsletters, annual reports, presentations, and public information resources. 13. *Coordinates timely preparation and submission of annual programmatic and fiscal reports, donor reports, continuation-funding documentation, and other required organizational reporting. 14. *Coordinates administrative relationships with governmental agencies, nonprofit organizations, Indigenous communities, partner institutions, professional organizations, donors, and other external stakeholders. 15. *Supports development and administration of partnership agreements, memoranda of understanding, briefing materials, and organizational communications. 16. *Develops and maintains administrative performance measures, tracking systems, dashboards, and organizational assessment processes supporting continuous improvement. 17. *Evaluates and implements administrative technologies, digital workflows, and operational innovations that improve organizational effectiveness and service delivery. 18. *Conducts research, environmental scans, administrative analyses, and special projects supporting Center planning, program development, and emerging ocean-policy opportunities. 19. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Function Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in public administration, business administration, education, public policy, marine science, environmental science, law or any related field and four (4) years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for program administration, project management, and/or organizational development; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of administrative and fiscal management, organizational development, and/or interdisciplinary program administration, as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with higher education and business administration, fiscal management, procurement, human resources, research support, and/or externally funded programs. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and [if applicable, add additional software]. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Desirable Qualifications 1. Master's degree in public administration, business administration, education, public policy, marine science, environmental science, law or any related field. 2. Experience supporting the establishment, growth, or organizational development of a new academic, research, or public-service program, center, or institute. 3. Progressively responsible experience administering complex programs involving multiple academic units, governmental agencies, nonprofit organizations, community partners, or international organizations. 4. Experience with higher education or research administration, including fiscal management, personnel coordination, procurement, travel, and development of administrative procedures. 5. Experience supporting strategic planning, organizational assessment, performance measurement, executive reporting, or continuous-improvement initiatives. 6. Experience administering graduate or professional student fellowships, experiential-learning programs, clinics, capstones, certificate programs, or related student initiatives. 7. Experience coordinating conferences, seminars, invited speakers, public programs, communications, websites, or external-engagement activities. 8. Knowledge of or professional experience related to ocean science, marine policy, environmental law, fisheries, coastal resilience, maritime governance, or Pacific Island issues. 9. Experience interpreting and implementing complex laws, regulations, policies, or compliance requirements in governmental, academic, research, or similarly regulated environments. 10. Experience with University of Hawaii fiscal, human resources, procurement, travel, student, research, or Foundation systems. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: (1) Cover letter to the Selection Committee indicating interest in the position and how the minimum and desirable qualifications of the position are met; (2) Resume; (3) Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of at least three professional references; and, (4) Official transcript(s) showing degree and coursework to date appropriate to the position (copies of official transcripts are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required at the time of hire.) Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account after clicking on the \"APPLY\" button. Inquiries: Richard Wallsgrove at wallsgro@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5428134/professor-0083413": {
      "title": "Professor (0083413)",
      "description": "Professor (0083413) Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Queen's Medical Center - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-01651 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Medicine Opening Date 07/28/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 83413 Description Title: Professor (H5M11) Position Number: 0083413 Hiring Unit: UHM John A. Burns School of Medicine, Department of Medicine Location: Queen’s Medical Center Date Posted: July 28, 2026 Closing Date: Rolling recruitment Salary Information: Salary commensurate with qualifications and experience. Monthly Type: 11 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: 100% Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Funding: G-funds Other Conditions: Full-time, tenure track, annually renewable depending on performance, departmental needs and/or funding availability. To begin approximately March 2027, subject to position clearance and availability of funds. First review begins August 19, 2026, with continuous recruitment until filled. This position may also be funded by extramural funds. Duties and Responsibilities: Participates in the development of principles and processes to guide the strengthening of academic programs in the department of medicine with respect to Undergraduate Medical Education (UME) and Graduate Medical Education (GME) program evaluation, and similar academic endeavors within John A. Burns School of Medicine (JABSOM) clinical affiliates. Assumes potential leadership roles as requested, which may include serving as Department Chair for Medicine and in a leadership role in an affiliated faculty practice medical group. Participate in strategic planning and implementation at the school level (e.g, relevant to the development of the department of medicine and JABSOM) and collaborate with the Queen’s University Medical Group (QUMG), Queen’s Health Systems (QHS), Hawai‘i Pacific Health (HPH), Hawai‘i Pacific Health Medical Group (HPHMG), and other JABSOM clinical affiliates, providing a unique academic clinical faculty and clinician scientist perspective to align clinical operations with the academic mission. Participates in developing clinical research and service programs that include JABSOM learners and faculty and are designed to strengthen the innovative delivery of health care within JABSOM clinical affiliates, with a focus on the disciplines of medicine. Assists with the maintenance of the Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME) and Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) for medicine and subspecialty experiences and programs based at all JABSOM clinical affiliates with such experiences. Oversees clinical quality improvement initiatives, curriculum development, and faculty evaluations. Serve as principal investigator for clinical services grant/contract proposals. Serve as mentor for students, residents, fellows, and faculty, and promote a strong clinical faculty development program based on H-series promotion goals. Promotes ongoing professional growth and development by participating in continuing medical education, leadership training, professional conferences, and other learning opportunities relevant to the practice of medicine and academic healthcare leadership. Conduct and publish research in areas affecting human health, patient care, or methods and techniques of medical education. Build a vigorous departmental research program with extramural funding. Engage in continuous learning through literature reviews, active participation in in-service meetings and workshops, and system-wide educational initiatives to stay current with academic best practices. Perform other related duties as assigned to support the mission and vision of the University of Hawai’i and JABSOM, in close coordination with the missions of JABSOM’s clinical affiliates. Perform related work as assigned by the Dean. Minimum Qualifications: MD degree or equivalent medical degree from an accredited medical school. Minimum of four years in the rank of Associate Professor, or ten years of other health care experience, or a total of ten years of teaching in the rank of Associate Professor and other health care experience, or one year in the rank of Professor at another health care school of recognized standing. Current Board certification in Internal Medicine with ongoing Maintenance of Certification, and any relevant subspecialty boards. Eligible for or currently holds an unrestricted Hawai’i Medical License. Evidence of excellence in teaching, national or international recognition as a scholar, and effective medical education and/or clinical program administration. Substantial track record of investigation and scholarship, including peer-reviewed publications in high-impact journals of relevant specialty. Demonstrated capacity for leadership in an academic Internal Medicine department. Ability to serve as a role model for students and junior colleagues. Excellent communication and team-building skills Desirable Qualifications: Experience working in a multicultural environment, including understanding the unique cultural aspects of Hawaii’s population. Documented participation in the scholarly and academic and/or clinical affairs of the medical school, university, professional organizations, and/or hospitals. Recognition as an outstanding academician, scholar, or clinician with a national and/or international reputation. Successful acquisition of funding for clinical trials, clinical services, or educational training grants and/or contracts. Demonstrated ability to lead a research/scholarship group or Center, provide research/scholarship training for junior faculty and/or graduate students, and/or lead research studies involving clinical trials, community-based research, quality improvement projects, or clinical services and intervention. Senior departmental or division leadership in a GME program, clinical division, service line, clinical program, or academic department, with evidence of mentoring faculty. Managerial experience in healthcare administration, medical management, or equivalent. Experience in strategic planning leading to division or departmental growth To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents: a cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, current curriculum vitae, Printout of your current American Board of Internal Medicine and any related subspecialty U.S. board certification, names of five (5) professional references (complete with contact information, including e-mail address). The application will be considered incomplete if any required documents are missing or unreadable. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Thomas Read, Chair, Department of Surgery tread6@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5540920/program-specialist-0097604": {
      "title": "Program Specialist (0097604)",
      "description": "Program Specialist (0097604) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02352 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Library Services Division Hamilton Library Opening Date 11/04/2026 Closing Date 11/25/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097604 Description Title: Program Specialist Position Number: 0097604 Hiring Unit: University of Hawai'i at Manoa, Library Services Location: UH at Manoa, Hamilton Library, Access Services Department Date Posted: 11/04/2026 Closing Date: 11/25/2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities (**denotes essential functions) 1. **Oversees the designated Access Services group to deliver seamless operations and continuous coverage across all public service points. 2. **Leads the recruitment, on boarding, scheduling, and evaluation of student assistants, collaborating with LT V staff. Reviews daily student reports to identify training needs or operational issues. 3. **Designs, implements, and updates training programs for staff and student assistants covering library policies, workflows, and technical hardware/software. Recommends, arranges and participates in professional development opportunities. 4. **Keeps abreast of emerging library technologies and standards. 5. **Conducts regular check-ins and formal annual performance evaluations for staff and student assistants. Develops performance improvement plans as needed and submit formal reports to Library Human Resources. 6. **Supports staff in managing difficult patron interactions. Assesses the issues and determines proper resolution such as authorizing policy exceptions, holding adjustments, and fee waivers within established guidelines. 7. **Maintains active engagement with daily operations ensuring proper coverage of the public service desk. 8. **Manages circulation services including access, circulation, and delivery services for library resources, including holds, digital document delivery, course reserves, AV media, and microfilm. 9. **Oversees Business Window operations and supervises the billing supervisor (LT V) in operations including collecting fines/fees, inputting financial transactions, and reconciling daily revenue reports. 10. **Monitors the student employee budget allotment and reports any discrepancies to the department chair and UHM Library Services Fiscal Office. 11. Plans, coordinates and manages special collection projects (inventory, shelf reading, shifting/relocation of materials) in collaboration with other departments. 12. **Periodically reviews and assesses circulation services, online system capabilities, and workflows, developing and recommending efficiencyimprovements. 13. **Supports the department chair in researching best practices at peer universities and recommends service additions, updates, or discontinuations to the department chair. 14. **In collaboration with the department chair, establishes, implements, and monitors standard operating procedures (SOPs) based on library policies and industry best practices. 15. In collaboration with the department chair, compiles statistics and prepares comprehensive operational reports on circulation functions and special projects. Leads regular meetings with unit supervisors and briefs the departmentchair on operational updates. 16. **Conducts periodic inspections of public facing service areas, including Circulation Counter, Business Office, AV/Microform Center, and study rooms, to ensure compliance with UH safety and quality standards. 17. Maintains an updated inventory of equipment for warranty and maintenance tracking. Processes repair or replacement requests for broken equipment, and maintain optimal supply levels for the department. 18. Oversees 3M Security Gate to ensure recording of daily counts, regular cleaning and arranges for service as needed. 19. Provides basic reference and library navigation assistance to patrons. 20. Supports general Access Services department tasks and special projects as needed. 21. Participates in UH system libraries Fulfilment Committee work. 22. Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Information Science, Communication, Humanities, or Social Sciences or related field, and 3 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for providing administrative support or supervision within public services, access services, collection management, or resource circulation in a library, educational, or information management environment; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of information management specific to library materials access and circulation services as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with the organizing and providing of access to information resources and library services. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide-ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Knowledge of library collection organization and management. 10. Knowledge of and experience in using or working in an academic library setting. 11. Knowledge and experience using an integrated library management system (Ex Libris or other similar programs). 12. Experience supervising and training employees and others in technical processes, specialized equipment, and software use. 13. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with demonstrated ability to work effectively and collegially with diverse individuals and groups. 14. Demonstrated ability to provide public-facing service transactions. 15. Proven ability to resolve complex problems using sound judgment, innovative reasoning, and effective prioritization. 16. Strong organizational, planning, and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines. 17. Experience preparing and monitoring budgets and reporting on allocated funds in compliance with established guidelines and governmental policies. 18. Ability to evaluate operations, analyze data, and make evidence-based recommendations for improvement. 19. Skill in drafting, proofreading, and editing accurate reports and procedural and training documentation for support staff and student assistants. Desirable Qualifications 1. Experience working within an academic community. 2. Familiarity with University of Hawai'i at Manoa Library systems, policies, and procedures. 3. Experience managing and supervising study room bookings, holds requests, course reserves. 4. Experience with using and managing microformats (Microfilm, microfiche) collections and viewing equipment. 5. Experience with fiscal/business transactions for the collection of fines and fees. 6. Proficiency with databases, spreadsheets, and office productivity software to compile, manage, and present data. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Submit cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, resume, names and contact information (including email addresses) of 3 professional references, and official transcripts (copies acceptable; however, originals will be required prior to completion of hiring). Incomplete applications will not be considered. All documentation becomes the property of the school. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Jean Thoulag, Screening Committee Chair and Department Chair, Access Services, thoulagj@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5480576/project-manager-0079085-ctahr-pmso": {
      "title": "Project Manager (#0079085) (CTAHR/PMSO)",
      "description": "Project Manager (#0079085) (CTAHR/PMSO) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-01966 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Planning and Management Systems Opening Date 09/13/2026 Closing Date 11/30/2026 12:00 AM Hawaii Position Number: 0079085 Description Questions Description Title: Project Manager Position Number: 0079085 Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resources (CTAHR), Office of the Dean, Office of Planning and Management Systems Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa, Honolulu, HI Date Posted: September 13, 2026 Closing Date : November 30, 2026, 11:59PM HST Band : B Salary : s alary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time : Full-time Month : 11-month Temporary/Permanent : Permanent Other Conditions : For best consideration, all application materials should be submitted by November 30, 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities *Manage facilities projects of various sizes and budgets through planning, design, procurement and construction to final completion at various geographical locations across the state. *Review, direct, and manage design consultants from project inception to completion. This includes but is not limited to site studies/space planning and reports, design drawings, plans, and specifications and/or any other related document for project completion prepared by consultants for compliance with Federal, State, City codes and regulations, this includes compliance to any University design requirements. *Provide technical expertise and oversight during all phases of projects. This includes but is not limited to preparing and/or reviewing project scoping, project schedules, project programming, cost and quantity estimates, bid documents, bid document amendments, bid analysis and award, bid and construction request for information (RFI) responses, Change Order analysis, consultant and contractor invoices, construction submittals and final walk through documents for the successful completion of a project. *Manage the construction of various repair, maintenance and renovations projects. *Ensure projects comply with UH's Design Guidelines, identify value-engineering ideas, actively observe construction and commissioning activities, visit construction sites and write observation reports, and confirm delivery of turnover-to-operation deliverables. *Conduct Owner-Architect-Contractor (OAC), pre-construction, inspection, final inspection and any other meetings as required for the successful completion of a project. *Design small remediation projects in-house, as well as manage and coordinate various third-party consultants to facilitate remediation projects of all sizes. *Provide technical assistance, recommendations, and architectural solutions to campus planning, operation, maintenance, and other University department units. *Troubleshoot and recommend solutions to resolve operational and maintenance problems. Coordinate with internal departments for building renewal projects and assessments, corrective engineering, preventative and deferred maintenance. *Work with Facilities in-house trade shops and UH's Environmental Health and Safety Office (EHSO) in efforts to make the Campus a safe work environment. *Work and communicate with various university department to identify building issues and its resolution/remediation. Establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders, members, and individuals. *Work and collaborate with the University's Office of Project Delivery (OPD) and Campus Operations and Facilities (COPF) on projects and work orders representing CTAHR's interests. *Participate in department operations planning and emergency response efforts. Utilize Trimble and/or other online project management software as needed for projects. Perform other duties as assigned. * Denotes essential functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Architecture, Architectural Engineering or Engineering and 5 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for design, construction, and/or contract administration related experience involving the application of industry principles and practices; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the field of design and construction as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal, city and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with site development and building design and construction, hazardous material testing and abatement. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply use of software related to project including but not limited to Bluebeam, Autocad, Revit, Word, Excel, Project, Trimble and Zoom (or any other video conference software). If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Able to prepare modifications to consultants and contractor contracts. Ability to design small remediation projects in-house, manage and coordinate various third-party consultants to facilitate remediation projects of all sizes. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Be familiar with testing procedures of applicable building codes. Demonstrated ability to perform hands-on forensic investigation of building enclosure failure or any other building failure in pursuit of remediation. Experience with existing condition analysis for compliance with accessibility regulation. Working knowledge of Hawaii regulations related to worker safety, including worker safety in high places. Ability and willingness to climb up ladders to get onto rooftops and look in to ceiling plenums, access tight utility spaces and other areas of buildings requiring assessment. Desirable Qualifications Registration as a licensed professional architect or engineer in the State of Hawaii. Possess the ability to learn or has knowledge of contract administration procedures and policies of the University and State of Hawaii. Additional certifications in the field of energy conservation design, historic preservation, accessibility for the disabled and worker safety are encouraged. (i.e. LEED). TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : CTAHR Human Resources, ctahrhr@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination on the basis of race, sex, gender identity and expression, age, religion, color, national origin, ancestry, citizenship, disability, genetic information, marital status, breastfeeding, income assignment for child support, arrest and court record (except as permissible under State law), sexual orientation, domestic or sexual violence victim status, national guard absence, or status as a covered veteran. For more information or inquiries regarding these policies, please refer to the following link: http://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/eeo-coordinators/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ 01 Do you possess a baccalaureate degree in Architecture, Architectural Engineering or Engineering or related field? Yes No 02 Do you have at least five (5) year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for design, construction, and/or contract administration related experience involving the application of industry principles and practices; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated? Yes No 03 Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you acknowledge that you understand that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5082831/psychologist-0097465-0097467": {
      "title": "Psychologist (0097465, 0097467)",
      "description": "Psychologist (0097465, 0097467) Location UH Manoa - Division of Student Success, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2025-02010 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - OVPSS - Counseling & Student Development Center Opening Date 09/17/2025 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0097465, 0097467 Description Title: Student Services Specialist (Psychologist) Position Number: 0097465, 0097467 Hiring Unit: OFC OF VP FOR STUDENT SUCCESS, STUDENT CO-CURRICULAR SUCCESS, COUNSL & STU DEV CTR Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: September 17, 2025 Closing Date: Continuous; Review of applications will begin on October 2, 2025 and will continue until the position is filled. Band: C Salary : Minimum starting salary at the PBC Step 5 ($86,040); commensurate with experience ( salary schedules and placement information ) Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Provide direct services to students which abides by established Counseling and Student Development Center (CSDC) policy and procedures, and includes but are not limited to: brief time-limited individual and group counseling for psychological academic and career concerns, phone screens and intake evaluations, crisis intervention and urgent drop-in services. 2. *Provide clinical case management duties for complex cases to include: connecting with relevant resources and providing on- and off-campus resources, collaborating with allied health care professionals related to treatments, gathering information and updating client charts. 3. *Respond to crises, suicide assessment, intervention and postvention cases. 4. Administer and interpret various assessment results (e.g., CCAPS, MBTI, SCII, LD/ADHD, MMPI) and prepare assessment reports in a timely manner. 5. *Provide individual clinical supervision, training and education. 6. Develop and implement outreach activities, workshops, and programming. 7. *Provide faculty, staff and student consultations and training where appropriate and when requested. 8. *Respond to campus and service requests for classroom presentations, talks, panels, information fairs, skills training, etc. 9. *Contribute and participate in CSDC staff development series and other professional activities. 10. Participate in Center, Division, and Campus-wide activities and programs. including leadership in those programs where interest and expertise are present. 11. Receive direction and guidance from the Director or designee. 12. Work closely with Center staff and campus colleagues as part of an integrated team. Participate collegially in meetings, teams and committees. 13. Work periodically on evenings, weekends and/or holidays as needed. 14. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Function Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a doctorate in Counseling Psychology, Clinical Psychology or combined psychology programs from an APA-accredited program or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for mental health support, assessment and intervention; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Advanced and comprehensive knowledge and understanding of a wide range of developing and emerging concepts, principles and methodologies in the area of clinical or counseling psychology. 3. Comprehensive knowledge and understanding of a wide range of principles, theories, federal and state laws and systems associated with the practice of psychology as a Licensed Psychologist. 4. Demonstrated ability to understand problems from a broad interactive perspective and use reasoning to discern underlying principles and issues when problem solving, and apply creative thinking to resolve problems in new and innovative approaches. 5. Demonstrated written and verbal competence in presenting ideas, concepts and models clearly using persuasion and negotiation to build consensus and cooperation. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and electronic medical recordkeeping system and other commonly used software. 8. For supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Completion of an APPIC-affiliated doctoral internship in health service psychology. 10. Current licensure as a psychologist in at least one state. 11. Must be able to obtain and maintain a Hawai`i Psychologist license within six months of start date. 12. Demonstrated experience to conduct brief psychotherapy, provide crisis intervention and delivery of group counseling. 13. Demonstrated experience to competently evaluate and intervene with mental health related concerns in young adults and adults. 14. Demonstrated experience in providing mental health consultation to professional and non-professional colleagues as well as parents and other concerned parties. 15. Evidence of strong skills in providing outreach on a variety of topics and prevention services. 16. Demonstrated proficiency in administering and interpreting various assessment results (e.g., CCAPS, MBTI, SCII, LD/ADHD, MMPI) and efficient production of assessment reports. 17. Demonstrated commitment and strong skills in working with individuals from varied backgrounds. 18. Ability to work outside of normal working hours, including evenings, weekends and holidays, as needed. Desirable Qualifications 1. Completion of an APA-accredited doctoral internship in health service psychology within a university counseling center. 2. Experience providing clinical supervision of graduate-level practicum trainees. 3. Evidence of coursework and/or competence in providing career counseling and interpreting career inventories. 4. Working knowledge and understanding of issues in Hawai`i. 5. Familiarity with traditional communities with varied backgrounds and student populations in Hawai`i. 6. Leadership in program development in one or more of the following areas: multicultural/intercultural programming, training and supervision of graduate level mental health trainees, group therapy, outreach, assessment, career counseling or peer support programs. 7. Evidence of strong skills in providing outreach on a variety of topics and prevention services related to the university population. To Apply: Click on the “APPLY” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: C over letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Current resume / curriculum vitae; Names and contact information (including telephone number and email addresses) of at least three (3) professional references; Copies of official undergraduate and graduate (as applicable) transcripts indicating degree conferred date with confidential information (i.e. social security number, date of birth) redacted. However, original official transcripts are required upon hire. Transcripts issued outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Incomplete, late or unreadable applications will not be considered. Academic record/grade summaries will not be accepted. Diplomas and copies will not be accepted. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Trystin Nakayama; trystin@hawaii.edu; 808-956-7927 EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529434/publications-specialist-editorial-design-and-production-manager-0080114": {
      "title": "Publications Specialist (Editorial, Design, and Production Manager) (0080114)",
      "description": "Publications Specialist (Editorial, Design, and Production Manager) (0080114) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02293 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Press Opening Date 10/26/2026 Closing Date 12/7/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0080114 Description Title: Publications Specialist (Editorial, Design, and Production Manager) Position Number: 0080114 Hiring Unit: UNIVERSITY OF HAWAII AT MANOA LIBRARY SERVICES, UNIVERSITY OF HAWAII PRESS, EDITORIAL, DESIGN, AND PRODUCTION DEPARTMENT Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa, UH Press (2840 Kolowalu Street, Honolulu, HI 96822) Date Posted: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 12/07/2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions) *Serves as the department head and primary representative for the Editorial, Design, and Production (EDP) Department. Manages the department's budget, planning, and organizational development. Manages the manufacturing and digital production workflow across multiple channels for new and backlist titles, ensuring high-quality execution of copyediting, proofreading, design, typesetting, print manufacture, and distribution. *Oversees planning for the the UH Press list by formulating budget estimates, tracking production costs, and executing final project closings for each book. *Establishes, documents, and maintains post-acquisition editorial and production standards while leading process optimization, communicating workflow changes, and ensuring procedural compliance across the department. Serves as the primary cross-functional liaison between internal staff, freelancers, and manufacturing vendors to facilitate a seamless printing and production process. *Directs the financial and operational workflow for each book project by generating accurate cost estimates, establishing and tracking budgets, and executing final project reconciliations. Drives strategic vendor management by negotiating and overseeing contracts for composition and external services, while routing individual projects based on cost, schedule, and vendor expertise. Identifies and pursues cost-savings optimization across all Editorial, Design, and Production (EDP) responsibilities to enhance both fiscal efficiency and final project quality. *Establishes long-term strategies and short-term departmental goals that balance editorial, production, marketing, and sales priorities against shifting industry trends. Advances operational excellence by developing and maintaining internal tools, templates, and systems that maximize EDP efficiency, consistency, and quality. *Conducts comparative analyses of vendors, peer presses, and industry standards: adapts workflows for print, digital, and e-formats while maintaining technical expertise in emerging hardware, software, and AI. Collaborates with the digital publishing manager to ensure departmental workflows plan for strict compliance with evolving accessibility regulations, including the ADA, Section 508, and the EU Accessibility Act. *Manages the daily operations of professional and technical design and production staff, alongside freelance providers, authors, and copyeditors, by assigning duties, setting performance goals, and providing constructive feedback. *Reports directly to the Press Director as an active member of the Press leadership team. Collaborates across departments including acquisitions, marketing, sales, journals, business, and digital publishing to advance shared organizational priorities. Communicates operational status, potential risks, and resource needs regularly to Press leadership. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in humanities or social sciences or related field and 6 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for book editorial design and production, and/or manufacturing management; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of book editorial design and production, and/or manufacturing management; as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with copyright and intellectual property as related the book publishing industry. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, including Microsoft Word, Microsoft Excel, and Adobe. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Familiarity with The Chicago Manual of Style and standard scholarly publishing conventions. At least 2 years in a supervisory position applying project management principles. Experience in fiscal management, including financial planning, castoffs and estimates, and preparing reports for management. Experience selecting, contracting, and managing vendor relationships, including negotiating terms, evaluating vendor performance, and routing work to appropriate vendors based on project requirements. Comprehensive knowledge of the technology and standard tools used across editorial, design , production, and digital workflows, including Creative Suite, XML editors, and editorial markup tools sufficient to valuate work and communicate effectively with staff and vendors. Desirable Qualifications Familiarity with UH procurement systems, rules and regulations. Advanced knowledge of Adobe InDesign and Photoshop. Knowledge of printing, digital technologies, and publishing procedures, including short-run digital printing, print on demand, EPUB, and accessibility standards. Working knowledge of accessibility laws and standards relevant to publishing (e.g., ADA, Section 508, EU Accessibility Act) and their implications for design, production, and digital deliverables. Proficiency with BiblioLive or comparable relational database application. To Apply Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents: (1) Cover letter that addresses how the applicant meets the minimum qualifications: degree requirements, experience, knowledge, skills, and abilities, as well as the desirable qualifications; (2) resume; (3) names and contact information (telephone numbers and email addresses) of at least three professional references; and, (4) official transcript(s) showing degree and coursework to date appropriate to the position (copies of official transcripts are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to completion of hiring). Late and/or incomplete (applications that do not include all four documents) will not be considered. All documents become the property of the school. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Clem Guthro, University Librarian and Interim Director and Publisher, UH Press, guthroc@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5475412/rainbow-wahine-water-polo-team-manager": {
      "title": "Rainbow Wahine Water Polo Team Manager",
      "description": "Rainbow Wahine Water Polo Team Manager Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Other Job Number 2026-01945 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 09/08/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: Pending Description Title: Rainbow Wahine Water Polo Team Manager Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Location: Women’s Water Polo Date Posted: September 8, 2026 Closing Date : Continuous recruitment until programmatic needs are met. Other Conditions: Continuous recruitment until programmatic needs are met. For best consideration, review of applications will begin September 16, 2026. Position renewable on availability of funds and satisfactory performance. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: CRITERIA FOR TEAM MANAGER: Per NCAA Bylaw 11.01.7 Manager: A manager is an individual who performs traditional managerial duties (e.g., equipment, laundry, hydration) and meet the following additional criteria: The individual shall be a full-time undergraduate (12 credits at the University of Hawaii at Manoa (UHM)) or graduate student (8 credits at UHM) (see Bylaws 14.2.2 and 14.2.2.1.4 ), except that during his or her final semester or quarter of a degree program, he or she may be enrolled in less than a full-time program of studies, provided he or she is carrying (for credit) the courses necessary to complete the degree requirements; The individual may participate in limited on-court or on-field activities during practice (e.g., assist with drills, throw batting practice) or competition (e.g., assist with warm-up activities) involving student-athletes on a regular basis; The individual shall not provide instruction to student-athletes; The individual shall not participate in countable athletically related activities (e.g., practice player) except as permitted in Bylaw 11.01.7 -(b); An undergraduate team manager must maintain a minimum semester and cumulative Grade Point Average (GPA) of 2.0 or above and complete a minimum of 12 credits each semester. A graduate team manager may be allowed to be a manager for two (2) years only. Graduate Manager must maintain a minimum semester and cumulative GPA of 3.0 or above. BENEFITS: Maximum amount a team manager may receive will be equal to 50% of an undergraduate resident tuition. Tuition does not include payment for Outreach College extension courses. Team Manager will be responsible for payment to Outreach College. Tuition payment will be prorated if the start date and completion of all required paperwork is after the first day of school. APPOINTMENT PERIOD : Fall 2026 to Spring 2026. Manager positions are renewable upon satisfactory performance, availability of funds and/or departmental assessment. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Acceptance and enrolled as a full-time student at the University of Hawai‘i at Manoa. Knowledge of water polo. Familiar with Microsoft Office Word and Excel, Google docs and other google platforms. Ability to communicate effectively. Ability to work independently. Good organizational and time management skills. Must be available to work an average of 20 hours per week. Ability to film, commentate and edit video. Passion about social media and professionalism for team's online reputation. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Required to possess and maintain a valid driver’s license. TO APPLY : Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete and application 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the qualifications 2) resume and 3) names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references. INQUIRIES: Head Water Polo Coach Ikaika Aki, andrewka@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5495116/readvertisement-admin-fiscal-support-specialist-0096970t": {
      "title": "READVERTISEMENT - Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist (0096970T)",
      "description": "READVERTISEMENT - Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist (0096970T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Queen's Medical Center - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02048 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Psychiatry Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0096970T Description READVERTISEMENT Title: Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist Position Number: 0096970T Hiring Unit: C OF HLTH SCI & SW, SCH OF MED, PSYCHIATRY DEPT Location: Queens Medical Center Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: November 16, 2026 at 11:59 PM HST Band: A Salary: salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBA, minimum $4,627/month Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Temporary, full-time, subject to position clearance and availability of funds. Position is annually renewable depending on performance and/or funding availability. Duties and Responsibilities *Provides administrative, fiscal, research, and educational support activities. *Performs procurement and fiscal actions, including the preparation of purchase orders, requisitions, invitations to bid, processes travel requests, p-card reallocation, requests for proposals/quotations or proposal/bid/technical specifications. Reviews and ensures the appropriateness and correctness of travel documents and processes for payment. Ensures that all goods and services are purchased in compliance with Federal, State and University guidelines and procedures. *Assist in personnel actions such as recruitment, on-boarding, and tracking of training and certification compliance, including for community providers/faculty non-compensated/volunteers. *Perform monthly reconciliation of project expenditures; generate monthly supporting documents ensuring compliance with funding agency's requirements. Maintain accurate account of project budget information and expenditures, generate financial report spreadsheets as necessary. Assist in processing and pursing grant and contracts. Assists with the preparation of a variety of reports. Anticipates, recognizes & resolves problems relating to the coordination of key management, operational, planning forums/meetings for the Department. Prepare and edit reports, agendas, letters, and complex documents such as quarterly reports that require specific formatting; and gather, interpret, and analyze information. Work with multiple agencies and affiliated partners at various locations. Supervision of staff, student hires, and/or volunteers as needed. Provides general office support in establishing and maintaining files, records and documents; answers general inquiries. *Coordinates continuing medical education (CME) activities as directed. Coordinates meetings/schedules and takes minutes. Performs other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of baccalaureate degree in administration, business, education, psychology, healthcare, or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area(s) of business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. Proven ability to quickly master complex concepts and methodologies, as demonstrated through academic coursework, projects, or self-directed study. Strong academic foundation with a proven capacity to learn new systems, tools, and processes with minimal guidance. Functional knowledge of current or previous financial or HR systems. Ability to learn and apply guidelines, policies, and procedures. Demonstrated ability to multi-task; assess situations to prioritize tasks based on importance and deadlines. Ability to travel independently in a timely and efficient manner, to various locations. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Desirable Qualifications 2 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, human resources/personnel, etc.), research administration, and/or educational administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the field of business, human resources, and/or fiscal demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Experience meeting requirements for multiple projects with multiple deadlines, which included effective organization, coordination, implementation, and communication. Experience working in human resources or business-related field performing administrative actions. Experience and working knowledge of the University of Hawai`i (UH) and the Research Corporation of the University of Hawai`i (RCUH) fiscal and human resources policies and procedures. Working experience with the University's electronic systems including but not limited to Kuali Financial System (KFS), RCUH Financial Portal, and Jasper Reporting Server (JRS). Experience using the UH myGRANT system. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents by the closing date: a cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications current resume official transcripts confirming degree completion (copies acceptable; however official transcripts sent directly from the degree granting institution required upon hire) Three (3) professional references (complete with contact information including e-mail address) The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Tiffinie Kiyota-Chan - KiyotaT@dop.hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5344541/readvertisement-assistant-professor-0085661t": {
      "title": "READVERTISEMENT - Assistant Professor (0085661T)",
      "description": "READVERTISEMENT - Assistant Professor (0085661T) Salary Depends on Qualifications Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Kaka'ako - Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-00989 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Tropical Medicine, Medical Microbiology and Pharmacology Opening Date 05/14/2026 Position Number: 0085661T Description Title: Assistant Professor (0085661T) Position Number: 0085661T Hiring Unit: John A. Burns School of Medicine (JABSOM), Department of Tropical Medicine, Medical Microbiology, and Pharmacology (TMMMP) Location: JABSOM Kakaako Date Posted: 6/10/2026 First Review: 6/25/2026 Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience. Monthly Type: 11 Month Tenure Track: Non-tenure track FullTime/PartTime: Part-Time (FTE .50) Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: To begin approximately July 1, 2026, or as soon thereafter as possible, pending position clearance and/or availability of funds. **The Department of Tropical Medicine, Medical Microbiology and Pharmacology (TMMMP), JABSOM, University of Hawaii at Manoa (UHM), seeks an Assistant Professor in the field of Tropical Medicine to lead and support the Department’s basic sciences, clinical and translational research in emerging and re-emerging infectious diseases, and to support the teaching/mentoring mission of the JABSOM. Assistant Professor – Duties and Responsibilities Teaching: 20% Teach credit courses in Tropical Medicine, Medical Microbiology and Pharmacology, and relevant disciplines offered by the Department to undergraduate, graduate, and medical students Serve as a mentor and advisor for undergraduate and graduate students, medical students, and residents. Research: 60% Participate in and support of existing infectious diseases research interests, while establishing/re-establishing own basic science, clinical or translational research program at JABSOM. Relevant research interests in the Department include infectious disease pathogenesis, host-microbe interactions, infectious disease epidemiology and diagnostics, clinical trials, and vaccine development for viral diseases, including HIV, SARS-CoV-2, dengue, West Nile virus, Zika, Filovirus; for parasitic diseases: malaria, rat lungworm. Collaborate with other principal investigators inside and outside the department engaged in various aspects of immunological and microbe-host interactions research. Submit applications for extramural funding. Present research findings or organize workshops at national and international conferences Conduct and publish research work in leading scholarly, peer-reviewed journal articles in the area of tropical medicine. Service: 20% Participate in Department’s committees for graduate admissions, assessment and curriculum development and evaluation. Participate in Department, School and University-wide committees, including serving on graduate committees, department and university committees, and the faculty senate. Other scholarly activities as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D., M.D., M.D./Ph.D., D.V.M. or other doctoral degree in the biosciences from a college or university of recognized standing. At least three (3) years of post doctoral research experience. Demonstrated ability in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Expertise in infectious diseases research with a strong research background as demonstrated by publication record in peer-reviewed journals. Demonstrated ability to obtain extramural research funding. Demonstrated scholarly achievement. Professionalism in meeting and conferring with others. Desirable Qualifications: Board certified or an active candidate for board certification and eligible for Hawaii medical license. Demonstrated experience in obtaining independent extramural research funding. Research interest in emerging and re-emerging infectious diseases. Experience in BSL-3 pathogens, animal models of BSL-3 pathogens, host-pathogen interactions. Experience in International research. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, CV Names and contact information of five (5) professional references; and Official transcripts (or original documentation from the institution confirming degree or copy of U.S. board certification or U.S. medical license). Copies of transcripts are accepted when used in lieu of current U.S. board certification or current U.S. medical license; however, if used for verification, original transcripts are required upon hire. The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not beconsidered. Inquiries: Dr. William Boisvert ( wab@hawaii.edu ) EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination inemployment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information,visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform andControl Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. Inaccordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campuscrime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus- security/, or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or AdministrativeServices Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities ActADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5481780/research-associate-regulatory-associate-0097177t-readvertisement": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Regulatory Associate) - (0097177T) - READVERTISEMENT",
      "description": "Research Associate (Regulatory Associate) - (0097177T) - READVERTISEMENT Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa - UH Cancer Center, Kaka'ako - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01969 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Opening Date 11/05/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097177T Description Benefits Description Title: Research Associate (Regulatory Associate) – (0097177T) - READVERTISEMENT Position Number: 0097177T Hiring Unit: RES & DEAN OF GRAD DIV, CANCER CT HI, CANCER CT HI Location: Honolulu, Kak'ako, Hawai'i Date Posted: November 5, 2026 Closing Date: November 16, 2026 Band: A Salary: salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBA, minimum $4,627 Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities: *Serves as the Regulatory Associate for the Clinical Trials Office (CTO), conducting, reviewing, and submitting clinical research studies through the study start-up process by preparing initial clinical research study applications and patient consent forms for submission to the respective Internal Review Board (IRBs) for local site activation of national cooperative group trials, pharmaceutical- sponsored trials, and investigator-initiated trials within the UH Cancer Center clinical trials network. Review all approved consents before studies are officially activated. *Maintains sponsor-written consent forms, log of local IRB reviews, protocol regulatory files of all IRB submissions and approvals, annual continuation renewal applications, sponsor amendments, and responds to IRB stipulations and inquiries to ensure document language is in compliance. Acts as a liaison with multiple IRBs. *Maintains data integrity by entering information accurately and timely in the Clinical Trials Management System (CTMS). Ensures appropriate research credentials are tracked and kept current for CTO staff, Translational Cancer Research (TCR) faculty and staff, and community investigators and staff. *Assist the Regulatory Supervisor with developing regulatory-related Clinical Research Regulatory Associate standard operating procedures, training, and documentation. *Assists frontline troubleshooting for all Hawaii Cancer Consortium/Network (i.e., community hospitals and clinics) staff at partnering institutions on regulatory-related issues. *Facilitates the review of Serious Adverse Events (SAEs) with research staff and PIs and communicates relevant SAE reports to IRBs. *Completes and maintains Delegation Task Logs when required by Sponsors and is responsible for regulatory duties on all ongoing clinical trials. Monitors the status of sponsor-issued protocol changes. *Contact study sponsors for assistance or seek additional information from the CTO Clinical Manager, principal investigators (PIs) and co-PIs, and other investigators regarding IRB queries. *Collaborates with CTO Clinical Manager, PIs, co-PIs, local PIs, lead Clinical Research Associates (CRAs), Compliance, Biocore, Investigator Initiative Trials (IIT) Manager, Protocol Coordinator, CTO Department Managers and Administrative staff, and other investigators for all IRB issues. *Partner with Compliance to maintain credentialing, audit preparation, and review of regulatory documents. *Works closely with UH HSR (Human Subjects Research), WIRB-Copernicus Group (WCG), Central Institutional Review Board, any other relevant IRBs, Office of Compliance, and IRB personnel from other Cancer Center programs, and Hawai`i Cancer Consortium institutions. *Communicates with the CTMS Manager and team for database changes, data quality assurance, and other CTMS tasks for accuracy to generate Clinical Research Regulatory Associate reports of clinical trial enrollments as mandated by the National Cancer Institute (NCI) and other sponsors. *Assists with maintaining an appropriate tracking system in CTMS for all protocol amendments/revisions and annual continuation renewals to ensure all federal regulations are met within the required parameters. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in health or related field and zero (0) year of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for clinical research; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the areas of practical and potential value of health care research, research process and regulatory requirements (Human Subject protection, conflict of interest, research misconduct) and working knowledge of medical terminology demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Functional knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with Clinical Research, regulatory protection program, regulatory procedures, HIPPA Compliance and regulations for research on human subjects. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of Clinical regulatory, Institutional review board (IRB) protocols, Clinical trials management systems (CTMS), and Human Subjects. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply work processing software, interpret informed consent forms and Google Suite, Microsoft Office and Adobe Acrobat. Supplemental Qualifications: Familiar with clinical trials and informed consent process. Desirable Qualifications: Demonstrated ability to work independently and take initiative on various projects. General knowledge of the University of Hawaii (UH) regulatory systems, research grants, and preparation of proposals and reports. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen and attach the required documents. Applicants must submit 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2) resume, 3) names and contact information of three (3) professional references and 4) official transcripts (OR original documentation from the institution confirming degree OR copy of U.S. board certification). Copy of transcript is acceptable in place of U.S. board certification for evaluation purposes but original is required upon hire. Transcripts issued outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA will be required before date of hire. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant (Note: Only complete applications will be considered.) NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: hr@cc.hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Address 701 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529171/research-support-staff-0097631t-ctahr-adsc": {
      "title": "Research Support Staff (0097631T; CTAHR/ADSC)",
      "description": "Research Support Staff (0097631T; CTAHR/ADSC) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02291 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Agricultural Diagnostic Service Center Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date 11/23/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097631T Description Benefits Questions Description Title : Research Support Staff (CTAHR/ADSC) Position Number(s): 0097631T Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), Agricultural Diagnostic Service Center (ADSC) Location: CARES Wailua, Kauai County; Kapaa, HI Date Posted : October 27, 2026 Closing Date : November 23, 2026, 11:59PM HST Band : A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time : Full-time Month : 11-month Temporary/Permanent : Temporary Other Conditions : This position is subject to availability of funding and renewal is contingent upon satisfactory performance, staffing requirements, and/or availability of funding. All application materials should be submitted by November 23 , 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities: *Coordinate technical logistics for setting up and maintaining field and laboratory trials evaluating crop performance, soil responses, and pest management systems as it relates to full cycle seed production. *Oversee scientific data logging systems and collect environmental parameters, soil metrics, water quality data, and plant growth measurements using scientific field instruments. *Coordinate the collection, cataloging, and preparation of soil, plant tissue, water, and microorganism samples for analytical laboratory processing. *Manages field isolation procedures in seed-production plots, monitoring spatial separation standards and buffer zones to safeguard genetic integrity, mitigate outcrossing risks, and preserve UH cultivar stock purity. *Oversees comprehensive seed quality assurance testing, managing multi-parameter analytical evaluations, including purity, moisture, and standardized viability trials, to guarantee strict alignment with state regulatory framework and HDOA requirements. *Oversees daily seed distribution logistics, monitoring inter-island shipments across multiple facility sites and leading bulk packaging operations to meet delivery schedules and quality standards. *Perform technical field evaluations to monitor pest/disease incidence and report physiological crop conditions to research project leads. *Log experimental trial parameters, field mapping coordinates, and observation data into specialized research databases and web-based decision tools. *Oversee daily technical operations and environmental schedules for research greenhouses, shade houses, hydroponic units, and/or field plots. *Program, troubleshoot, and calibrate automated irrigation networks, environmental control computers, and specialized research facility equipment. *Coordinate research plot layout schedules, treatment application timing, and technical equipment setup for research trials. *Inspect research facilities, automated environmental controls, and field instrumentation to perform specialized technical maintenance and schedule major facility repairs. *Maintain detailed operational logs, chemical/pesticide application tracking, inventory records, and facility usage schedules. *Manage procurement logistics for specialized research supplies, fertilizers, field tools, and safety gear. *Monitor field and facility compliance with state, federal, and University environmental health, safety, and worker protection standards. *Assist research personnel in compiling trial summary tables, preparing field demonstration plot layouts, and drafting technical documentation. * Support time-critical research trial requirements, crop maintenance, facility operational monitoring, and other research functions, which may require working outside of normal business hours as needed. *Transports equipment and materials for research to various locations. Perform other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Botany, Biology, Horticulture or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for N/A; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and agricultural research, environmental management, and/or program management software. Ability of active listening skills and the ability to work independently across multiple locations, and flexibility to adapt to changing research and/or operational needs. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirements may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Ability to work outside of normal business, including early mornings, evenings, weekends, and/or holidays as needed. Ability to lift, carry, or otherwise move supplies and equipment up to 40 pounds, with or without assistance or utilizing appropriate methods (e.g., carts, dollies, or team lifts). Desirable Qualifications Experience supporting agricultural research operations, field trial logistics, greenhouse operations, and/or biological research data collection (or equivalent combination of education and experience). Working knowledge of technical methods in agronomy, horticulture, and/or soil science, to include operation of controlled environment facilities, standard pest and/or disease identification, and/or scientific data logging and/or record-keeping procedures. Ability to coordinate technical field research tasks and maintain accurate data logs. Ability to operate and program automated environmental/irrigation systems. Ability to enforce safety protocols for chemical application and facility operation. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Dr. Kevin Olival, kolival@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits 01 Do you have a baccalaureate degree in Botany, Biology, Horticulture or related field? Yes No 02 Complete applications and all requested document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you attest that you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5523577/research-support-staff-0097695t-ctahr-cares-kauai": {
      "title": "Research Support Staff (0097695T; CTAHR/CARES/KAUAI)",
      "description": "Research Support Staff (0097695T; CTAHR/CARES/KAUAI) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Kapaa, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02262 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Agricultural Diagnostic Service Center Opening Date 10/21/2026 Closing Date 11/11/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097695T Description Benefits Questions Description Title : Research Support Staff (CTAHR/CARES/KAUAI) Position Number: 0097695T Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), Kauai County Location: CARES Wailua, Kauai County; Kapaa, HI Date Posted : October 21, 2026 Closing Date : November 11, 2026, 11:59PM HST Band : A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time : Full-time Month : 11-month Temporary/Permanent : Temporary Other Conditions : This position is subject to availability of funding and renewal is contingent upon satisfactory performance, staffing requirements, and/or availability of funding. All application materials should be submitted by November 11 , 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities: *Coordinate technical logistics for setting up and maintaining field and laboratory trials evaluating crop performance, soil responses, and pest management systems. *Oversee scientific data logging systems and collect environmental parameters, soil metrics, water quality data, and plant growth measurements using scientific field instruments. *Coordinate the collection, cataloging, and preparation of soil, plant tissue, water, insects and microorganism samples for analytical laboratory processing. *Perform technical field evaluations to monitor pest/disease incidence and report physiological crop conditions to research project leads. *Log experimental trial parameters, field mapping coordinates, and observation data into specialized research databases and web-based decision tools. *Oversee daily technical operations and environmental schedules for research greenhouses, shade houses, hydroponic units, and/or field plots, located at various geographical locations and require monitoring outside of normal business hours such as early mornings, evenings, weekends and/or on holidays. . *Program, troubleshoot, and calibrate automated irrigation networks, environmental control computers, and specialized research facility equipment. *Coordinate research plot layout schedules, treatment application timing, and technical equipment setup for research trials. *Inspect research facilities, automated environmental controls, and field instrumentation to perform specialized technical maintenance and schedule major facility repairs. *Maintain detailed operational logs, chemical/pesticide application tracking, inventory records, and facility usage schedules. *Manage procurement logistics for specialized research supplies, fertilizers, field tools, and safety gear. *Monitor field and facility compliance with state, federal, and University environmental health, safety, and worker protection standards. *Assist research personnel in compiling trial summary tables, preparing field demonstration plot layouts, and drafting technical documentation. *Transports equipment and materials for research. Other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Agriculture, Agronomy, Horticulture, Soil Science, Biological Sciences, or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities in N/A; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and agricultural research, environmental management, and/or program management software. Ability of active listening skills and the ability to work independently across multiple locations, and flexibility to adapt to changing research and/or operational needs. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirements may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Ability to work outside of normal business, including early mornings, evenings, weekends, and/or holidays as needed. Ability to lift, carry, or otherwise move supplies and equipment up to 40 pounds, with or without assistance or utilizing appropriate methods (e.g., carts, dollies, or team lifts). Desirable Qualifications Experience supporting agricultural research operations, field trial logistics, greenhouse operations, or biological research data collection (or equivalent combination of education and experience). Working knowledge of technical methods in agronomy, horticulture, and/or soil science, to include operation of controlled environment facilities, standard pest and/or disease identification, and/or scientific data logging and/or record-keeping procedures. Ability to coordinate technical field research tasks and maintain accurate data logs. Ability to operate and program automated environmental/irrigation systems. Ability to enforce safety protocols for chemical application and facility operation. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Dr. Valerie Barko, vabarko@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits 01 Do you have a baccalaureate degree in Agriculture, Agronomy, Horticulture, Soil Science, Biological Sciences, or related field? Yes No 02 Complete applications and all requested document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you attest that you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5523575/research-support-staff-0097729t-0097730t-ctahr-adsc": {
      "title": "Research Support Staff (0097729T & 0097730T; CTAHR/ADSC)",
      "description": "Research Support Staff (0097729T & 0097730T; CTAHR/ADSC) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02261 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Agricultural Diagnostic Service Center Opening Date 10/21/2026 Closing Date 11/18/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097729T & 0097730T Description Benefits Questions Description Title : Research Support Staff (CTAHR/ADSC) Position Number(s): 0097729T; 0097730T Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), Agricultural Diagnostic Service Center (ADSC) Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa; Honolulu, HI Date Posted : October 21, 2026 Closing Date : November 18, 2026, 11:59PM HST Band : A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time : Full-time Month : 11-month Temporary/Permanent : Temporary Other Conditions : This position is subject to availability of funding and renewal is contingent upon satisfactory performance, staffing requirements, and/or availability of funding. All application materials should be submitted by November 18 , 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities: *Coordinate technical logistics for setting up and maintaining research trials evaluating crop performance, nutrient use efficiency, and pest and disease management protocols in hydroponics- and aquaponics-based growing systems under greenhouse and/or screenhouse conditions. *Oversee scientific data logging systems and collect environmental parameters, soil metrics, water quality data, and plant growth measurements using scientific field instruments. *Coordinate the collection, cataloging, and preparation of soil, plant tissue, water, insects and microorganism samples for analytical laboratory processing. *Perform technical field evaluations to monitor pest/disease incidence and report physiological crop conditions to research project leads. *Log experimental trial parameters, field mapping coordinates, and observation data into specialized research databases and web-based decision tools. *Oversee daily technical operations and environmental schedules for research greenhouses, shade houses, hydroponic units, and/or field plots, located at various geographical locations and require monitoring outside of normal business hours such as early mornings, evenings, weekends and/or on holidays. *Program, troubleshoot, and calibrate automated irrigation networks, environmental control computers, and specialized research facility equipment. *Coordinate research plot layout schedules, treatment application timing, and technical equipment setup for research trials. *Inspect research facilities, automated environmental controls, and field instrumentation to perform specialized technical maintenance and schedule major facility repairs. *Maintain detailed operational logs, chemical/pesticide application tracking, inventory records, and facility usage schedules. *Manage procurement logistics for specialized research supplies, fertilizers, field tools, and safety gear. *Monitor field and facility compliance with state, federal, and University environmental health, safety, and worker protection standards. *Assist research personnel in compiling trial summary tables, preparing field demonstration plot layouts, and drafting technical documentation. *Transports equipment and materials for research. Work with and support high school, undergraduate and graduate students engaged in internships and hands-on learning activities. Other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Agriculture, Agronomy, Horticulture, Soil Science, Biological Sciences, or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities in N/A; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and agricultural research, environmental management, and/or program management software. Ability of active listening skills and the ability to work independently across multiple locations, and flexibility to adapt to changing research and/or operational needs. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirements may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Ability to work outside of normal business, including early mornings, evenings, weekends, and/or holidays as needed. Ability to lift, carry, or otherwise move supplies and equipment up to 40 pounds, with or without assistance or utilizing appropriate methods (e.g., carts, dollies, or team lifts). Desirable Qualifications Experience supporting agricultural research operations, field trial logistics, greenhouse operations, or biological research data collection (or equivalent combination of education and experience). Working knowledge of technical methods in agronomy, horticulture, and/or soil science, to include operation of controlled environment facilities, standard pest and/or disease identification, and/or scientific data logging and/or record-keeping procedures. Ability to coordinate technical field research tasks and maintain accurate data logs. Ability to operate and program automated environmental/irrigation systems. Ability to enforce safety protocols for chemical application and facility operation. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Dr. Parwinder Grewal, pgrewal@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits 01 Do you have a baccalaureate degree in Agriculture, Agronomy, Horticulture, Soil Science, Biological Sciences, or related field? Yes No 02 Complete applications and all requested document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you attest that you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
