    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5376866/warrior-football-student-team-manager": {
      "title": "Warrior Football Student Team Manager",
      "description": "Warrior Football Student Team Manager Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01268 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 06/13/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: Pending Description Title: Warrior Football Student Team Manager Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Department Location: Football Program Date Posted: June 13, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous - application review begins June 22, 2026 Duties and Responsibilities: Assist with all managerial aspects of the program including clerical work such as inputting of data and filing paperwork, assist with equipment and uniforms by preparing the equipment and uniforms for set-up and clean-up for practice, home and away games. Demonstrate effective organizational skills and time management. Incumbent must demonstrate a positive and professional working attitude and maintain collaborative working relationship with the coaching staff, team members, support staff, administrators in the UH Athletics Department and other individuals associated with the Rainbow Warrior Football Program. Responsible for equipment and uniform set-up and clean-up for practice, home and away game facilities for the Rainbow Warrior Football Team. Packing of equipment for away games. The individual may participate in limited on-field activities during practice (e.g., assist with drills) or competition (e.g., assist with warm-up activities). Clock management during practices. Responsible for general clerical office work and record keeping. Inputting of data and filing paperwork. Maintain confidentiality with office work and all other aspects of the program. Assist with checking of student-athlete equipment to ensure its safety. Be available prior, during and after all home competitions for game preparation and other duties related to the event. Comply with the Rainbow Warrior Football Team Guidelines. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Know, recognize, and comply with laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and the affiliated Conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if there are reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees, students, or others, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. CRITERIA FOR TEAM MANAGER: An undergraduate team manager must maintain a minimum semester and cumulative Grade Point Average (GPA) of 2.0 or above and complete a minimum of 12 credits each semester. A graduate team manager may be allowed to be a manager for two (2) years only. Graduate Manager must maintain a minimum semester and cumulative GPA of 3.0 or above and complete a minimum of 8 credits each semester. Managers must be able to work up to 20-30 hours per week. BENEFITS: Maximum amount a team manager may receive will be equal to an undergraduate resident tuition. Tuition does not include payment for Outreach College extension courses. Team Manager will be responsible for payment to Outreach College. Tuition payment will be prorated if the start date and completion of all required paperwork is after the first day of school. APPOINTMENT PERIOD: Fall 2026 semester. Manager positions are renewable upon satisfactory performance and availability of funds. Minimum Qualifications: Acceptance and enrolled as a full-time student at the University of Hawai‘i at Manoa. Knowledge of football and football equipment Familiar with Microsoft Office Word and Excel Ability to communicate effectively Ability to work independently Ability to manage time wisely. Must be available to work an average of 30 hours per week. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete and application 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the qualifications 2) resume and 3) names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references. Inquiries : Al Ginoza, Equipment Manager; ginozaa@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5541971/admin-fiscal-support-spec-0097090": {
      "title": "Admin & Fiscal Support Spec (0097090)",
      "description": "Admin & Fiscal Support Spec (0097090) Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02356 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 11/05/2026 Closing Date 11/20/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097090 Description Title: Admin & Fiscal Support Spec Position Number: 0097090 Hiring Unit: INT ATHLETICS UHM, INT ATHLETICS UHM, INT ATHLETICS UHM Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: November 5, 2026 Closing Date: November 20, 2026 Band: A Salary: salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions) *Under the supervision of the Associate Athletics Director for Student-Athlete Services, provides administrative support to the Associate Athletics Director on a wide variety of administrative responsibilities and works directly with coaching staff of assigned sports. *Provides office management including but not limited to establishing office procedures, scheduling meetings, greeting/screening guests, determining disposition of incoming correspondence/mail, preparing documents, and developing/maintaining an effective filing system. *Coordinates and ensures that team and recruiting travel arrangements are prepared, completed and meet coach and team requirements (e.g. scheduling, booking hotel accommodations, and event logistics). *Responsible for preparation of travel documents for assigned sports and programs. Prepares requisitions, reconciles and completes documents upon traveler's return. Ensures compliance with UH policies and procedures and NCAA rules and regulations. *Responsible for processing fiscal transactions of assigned sport's and program purchases. *Responsible for processing and recording deposits and expenses to the UH Foundation for assigned sports and programs ensuring compliance with UH and UH Foundation fiscal policies and procedures. *Assists with the review, analysis and preparation of administrative, fiscal and personnel related documents as requested by the Associate Athletics Director. *Assists the Associate Athletics Director with student-athlete support services involving confidential information, may involve contact with student-athlete, administration, coaches, staff and colleagues from other institutions, conferences and external entities. *Assists Director of Student Athlete Success in preparation and distribution of documents related to scholarships, stipends and other student related services (e.g. Alston Academic Awards program and Financial Aid agreements). Drafts pertinent documents including action memos and responds to internal/external inquiries as needed. *Maintains and updates various lists of activities and contacts for Athletic Department including committee lists, administrative lists and other mailing and/or distribution lists. *Coordinates Associate Athletic Director's master calendar and related arrangements. Schedules appointments and meetings, coordinates and maintains calendars, makes arrangements for travel and accommodation and processes travel expense reports. *Assists Associate Athletics Director to address and/or answer concerns and inquiries expressed by individuals within the University community and general public, and assists in determining appropriate action or response in a timely and professional manner. *Assists Executive Team (Athletics Director, Associate Athletics Directors and Assistant Athletics Directors) with administrative support as assigned. *Assists with special assignments, projects and/or events as assigned (e.g., Financial Literacy program, Welcome Back Pau Hana, H Awards), which may require working flexible hours including early mornings, evenings, weekends and/or holidays. Coordinates logistics including but not limited to scheduling facilities, invitations, arranging food/beverage, and coordinating guest reception and various logistics to meet the goals and objectives of the project. Keeps abreast of changes in athletics affairs and changes in applicable laws, policies and procedures. May supervise graduate student assistants and student assistants. *Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. *Maintains collaborative working relationships with administrators and coaches in the department, other college offices, and appropriate relationships with members of the media, donors, and other individuals interested in the Athletics Department. *Knows, recognizes and complies with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawai'i, its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated Conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Athletics Director or designee if there is reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees, students, or others, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Arts and Sciences, Business Administration, Humanities, Social Sciences, Natural Sciences, Education or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for program administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of program administration demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with program administration. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of program administration. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and spreadsheet software. Ability to exercise discretion and sound analyses to a variety of administrative support, program management or related issues. Ability to work effectively and independently under pressure, with frequent interruptions, and remain flexible in handling several concurrent projects with competing deadlines. Must be able to work a flexible schedule including nights, weekends and holidays, depending on programmatic needs. Ability to handle confidential information with a high level of tact, discretion and diplomacy. Excellent organizational and time management skills. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Demonstrated ability to pay attention to details. Desirable Qualifications Experience in intercollegiate athletic administration. Knowledge of NCAA rules and regulations. Knowledge of University of Hawaii organizations, applicable policies and procedures, and relevant practices. Experience with University of Hawaii on-line systems, including, but not limited to Kuali Financial System and eTravel. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number). Inquiries : Laura Beeman, Associate Athletics Director for Student-Athlete Success, lbeeman@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5542472/assistant-professor-conservation-biology-0084189": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Conservation Biology (0084189)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Conservation Biology (0084189) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02366 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - School of Life Sciences Opening Date 11/05/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0084189 Description Title: Assistant Professor - Conservation Biology Postion Number: 0084189 Location: UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type: Full-Time Permanent Department: University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - School of Life Sciences Date posted: November 5, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous - first review of applicants will take place on January 12, 2027 Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Yes Full/Part Time: Full time Temp/Perm: Permanent Funding: General Funds Position Description The School of Life Sciences (SoLS) at the University of Hawai‘i at Manoa seeks a candidate for an Assistant Professor (F3) of Conservation Biology. Research addressing conservation challenges in Hawai'i, including its native species, ecosystems, and biodiversity, is of particular interest. The successful candidate can have research experience in any area of conservation biology. This is a permanent, tenure-track, full-time, 9-month faculty appointment to begin August 1, 2027, subject to position clearance, availability of funds, and satisfactory performance. The University of Hawai‘i at Manoa (UHM) is a Carnegie Research 1 University with strong emphases on research and undergraduate and graduate education. Our vision is to be locally and globally recognized as a premier student-centered and community-serving university ( https://manoa.hawaii.edu/ strategicplan/ ). As part of our strategic mission, we strive to become a Native Hawaiian Place of Learning, a place that is committed to honoring Native Hawaiian ways of knowing and including Native Hawaiian language, culture, and values into its diverse academic and campus environments. The School of Life Sciences hosts the largest academic program on the University of Hawai‘i’s flagship campus, serving over 1,500 undergraduate majors and 150 graduate students for degrees in biology, botany, marine biology, microbiology, molecular cell biology, and zoology. Research interests of the 40 faculty members span all biological scales, with many focusing on ecology, evolution, and conservation biology. The School of Life Sciences is committed to serving our community and state and to excellence in research and teaching. ( https://manoa.hawaii.edu/ lifesciences/ ). Duties and Responsibilities - Develop and sustain an innovative, externally funded research program - Mentor and advise graduate and undergraduate students in the School of Life Sciences - Provide high-quality teaching in support of the School’s undergraduate degree programs - Develop and teach high-quality graduate courses in their area of expertise that directly contribute to one or more of the graduate degrees offered by the School of Life Sciences - Serve on departmental, college, and university committees as appropriate - Render service to the professional and local/regional community that is relevant to the individual’s academic specialty Minimum Qualifications - A Ph.D or equivalent terminal degree from a college or university of recognized standing in a field relevant to the position by the time of hire - Research experience in conservation biology or a related field - Excellence and creativity in research, as demonstrated by publications in peer-reviewed journals - Ability to teach and mentor a diversity of students - Commitment to supporting the key principles of the School of Life Sciences, which include personal and professional integrity and the pursuit of excellence in instruction and research - Professionalism in meeting and conferring with others Desirable Qualifications - Postdoctoral research experience - Evidence of external research funding - Demonstrated experience and excellence in the instruction of students in the life sciences, with an emphasis on conservation biology - Experience working with a diversity of students - A vision of research that draws inspiration from Hawai‘i’s uniqueness and complements faculty expertise in the School of Life Sciences, other university units, and state and federal agencies engaged in conservation biology - Experience conducting, or clear commitment to developing, biocultural conservation research that is linked to place-based community-led and culturally informed conservation efforts - Research in fields related to ornithology, plant-animal interactions, or other conservation biology topics that span multiple disciplinary interests across the School of Life Sciences To Apply Applicants should submit: 1) a cover letter in which you identify the position for which you are applying and indicate how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a statement of research interests, activities, and plans (up to 3 pages in length); 4) a statement of teaching philosophy, interests, and plans (up to 2 pages in length); 5) copies of up to three relevant publications; and 6) names and contact information (including email addresses and telephone numbers) for three professional references. Additional information about UH Manoa’s Strategic Vision as a Native Hawaiian place of learning can be found here: https://manoa.hawaii.edu/ strategicplan/ . Official transcripts with proof of degree conferred will be required upon hire. Inquiries: Dr. Floyd Reed, floydr@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5542496/assistant-professor-in-early-childhood-leadership-curriculum-and-pedagogy-00826": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Early Childhood Leadership, Curriculum and Pedagogy (0082632)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Early Childhood Leadership, Curriculum and Pedagogy (0082632) Location UH Manoa - College of Ed, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02367 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education Division Curriculum Studies Opening Date 11/05/2026 Position Number: 0082632 Description Title: Assistant Professor in Early Childhood Leadership, Curriculum and Pedagogy Position Number: 0082632 Hiring Unit: College of Education, Department of Curriculum Studies Location: Manoa Date Posted: November 5, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous - application review begins December 2, 2026 Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: General funds, contingent upon position clearance, department needs, and fund availability. Appointment to begin August 202 7. Selectee is required to complete the COE New Faculty Orientation in the first year, successfully pass a criminal background check, fulfill all mandatory training and compliance requirements and maintain an on-campus presence. Duties and Responsibilities Develop and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in Curriculum Studies in face-to-face, online, and hybrid formats, as determined by departmental needs. Coordinate cohorts and conduct field supervision for the MEd-Early Childhood Education, Early Childhood Education Graduate Certificate , and other Curriculum Studies graduate programs. Contribute to the development of ECE program in collaboration with the School of Teacher Education and Special Education. Hold regular in-person office hours to support student learning and engagement. Provide expertise for early childhood education leadership, curriculum, and pedagogy. Advise and mentor graduate students. Contribute to recruitment & admissions, program and curriculum development, assessment, and accreditation activities for the ongoing sustainability and improvement of graduate ECE program offerings. Contribute as part of interdisciplinary/interagency teams to building a strong early learning infrastructure that addresses Hawai’i’s ECE workforce needs. Maintain an active program of research/scholarly activity and publications. Participate in accreditation processes, including documentation and report preparation. Attend in-person departmental and/or college meetings. Engage in grant and extramural proposal writing to support research and program activities. Engage in service to the department, college, university, and community. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Earned doctorate in early childhood education or closely related field from an accredited institution (ABD candidates will be considered if the degree is conferred by date of hire). Strong theoretical and methodological background in early childhood education curriculum and pedagogy, public policy, systems building, collaborative leadership, and ECE workforce development that advances the birth-to-eight systems. 1 year of experience working with infants, toddlers and/or preschoolers, as well as knowledge of children from birth to eight in multiple contexts (e.g., family child care, center-based child care, Early Head Start/Head Start, public school early childhood settings, and Family-Child Interactive Learning programs) . 1 year of experience with early childhood education advocacy for children and families at the policy level. 1 year of experience teaching at the post-secondary level. Evidence of emerging scholarly productivity (e.g., research publications, conference presentations, grant writing, or extramural funding). Any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience that provides the required knowledge, skills, and abilities indicated. Desirable Qualifications 1 year of experience working with Hawai‘i’s varied populations, and Hawai‘i’s early learning systems. 1 year of experience engaging in place-based early childhood education teaching and research with community partners. 1 year of experience in ECE graduate program development, administration, licensure, assessment and accreditation activities. Knowledge of ECE standards and initiatives. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents to the online application. The applicants should submit the following documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications Curriculum vitae Names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references, Official transcripts (copies are acceptable with application; official transcripts are required upon hire) 1-3 reprints of scholarly publications Late, incomplete, or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Please REDACT references to social security numbers and birth date on submitted documents. All items become the property of the University of Hawai‘i and will not be returned. NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. The maximum file size to upload/attach is 10 MB. If the attachment is larger than 10 MB, please split the file and upload it as a supplemental attachment. Inquiries: Kimo Cashman; kcashman@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5542086/fiscal-specialist-0081464": {
      "title": "Fiscal Specialist (0081464)",
      "description": "Fiscal Specialist (0081464) Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02358 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 11/05/2026 Closing Date 11/20/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0081464 Description Title: Fiscal Specialist Position Number: 0081464 Hiring Unit: INT ATHLETICS UHM, INT ATHLETICS UHM, INT ATHLETICS UHM Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: Closing Date: Band: A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions) *Assists with the daily fiscal operations of the Athletic Business Office. *Assists with the reviewing and executing reimbursements for recruiting visits in coordination with the Compliance Office and in accordance with NCAA rules. *Assists with reviewing, approving and executing payment documents for departmental purchases, including but not limited to BSN/Nike, Outrigger, other vendors, NCAA, Conference. *Monitors and administers fixed assets (including disposal applications and adjustment) in eKuali Financial System (eKFS) and related reports. *Administers game contest contracts and payment of guarantees. Process and coordinate non-cash game contract guarantees (i.e., issue purchase orders for airfare and/or hotel accommodation, informs Ticket Office should there be ticket allocation guarantees, etc). *Serves as trade coordinator with administering, coordinating, and tracking the use and balances of in-kind corporate partner trade agreements including but not limited to air, hotels, restaurants. *Assists with processing a wide range of eKFS transactions, including user access authorization, travel and expense reimbursements, and purchase requisitions. *Reviews and executes scholarship forms to student-athletes for monthly stipend and book reimbursements. *Responds to general inquiries on purchasing and reimbursement procedures including but not limited to eKFS, ARMS, Certificate of Vendor Compliance. *Creates vendor codes for eKFS transactions. Coordinate with requesting departments to ensure accurate information is reflected in the vendor code profile. Assist with training new departmental personnel and/or student employees. *Maintains collaborative working relationships with administrators and coaches in the department, other college offices, and appropriate relationships with members of the media, donors, and other individuals interested in the Athletics Department. *Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. *Knows, recognizes, and complies with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. *Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if there is reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees or students, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in business administration, accounting or finance or related field and 1 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for financial accounting and/or fiscal/procurement administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of business administration demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with business administration. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the business administration environment. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing and spreadsheet software. Desirable Qualifications Experience with University on-line systems, (eKuali Financial System (eKFS) and Teamworks). Knowledge of University of Hawai`i organizations, applicable policies and procedures, and relevant practices. Experience in intercollegiate athletics administration. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number). Inquiries : Tiffany Kuraoka, Assistant Athletics Director for Business Operations, tkuraoka@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5485094/instructional-student-supp-spec-office-of-student-services-coord-recruitment": {
      "title": "Instructional & Student Supp Spec (Office of Student Services Coord & Recruitment Support) 0078875T",
      "description": "Instructional & Student Supp Spec (Office of Student Services Coord & Recruitment Support) 0078875T Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01990 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene Opening Date 11/05/2026 Closing Date 11/20/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0078875T Description Title: Instructional & Student Support Specialist (Office of Student Services Coordinator) Position Number: 0078875T Hiring Unit: School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene Location: Manoa Campus / Honolulu Date Posted: November 5, 2026 Closing Date: November 20, 2026 Band: A Minimum Monthly Salary: Salary Schedules and Placement Information Full Time/Part Time: Full-Time, 100% Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Position is temporary with the possibility of conversion to permanent , subject to position clearance and availability of funds. This position will support the operations of the University of Hawaii at Manoa School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene (SONDH) Office of Student Services, and is expected to begin approximately December 2026 or as mutually acceptable. Renewal of appointment is dependent on performance, program needs and/or availability of funds. To know more about the UH at Manoa, SONDH, go to: https://nursing.hawaii.edu/ . Duties and Responsibilities: * Manages day-to-day operations of the Office of Student Services (OSS) Front Desk, serving as the primary point of contact for students and visitors. Provides a welcoming environment, answers general school/program inquiries, responds to phone calls, sorts mail, manages office supplies, maintains bulletin boards and reception cleanliness, and directs complex concerns to appropriate advisors/coordinators. * Provides administrative and fiscal support to the OSS Director and overall unit. * Maintains individual student records in the student database accurately and up to date. * Verifies enrollment and graduation for licensure, employment, and certifications to the Board of Nursing and educational institutions at the instruction of the OSS Director. * Ensures all student information released complies with FERPA guidelines and University of Hawaii policies and procedures. * Develops and maintains a comprehensive office manual of OSS administrative policies and procedures, implementing new processes and services at the direction of the OSS Director. * Provides comprehensive recruitment and enrollment outreach support to promote SONDH academic programs and meet enrollment goals. Organizes, schedules, and provides logistical support for recruitment events, orientations, informational sessions, feedback forums, summits, and outreach activities (including those outside normal business hours/weekends). * Assists in developing, preparing, updating, and coordinating the distribution of recruitment and outreach materials across various platforms, including websites, email, presentations, digital platforms, and printed brochures. * Collaborates and partners with internal OSS team members, faculty, staff, and external campus and community partners (including Admissions, Records, and Financial Aid) to support recruitment initiatives, prospective student engagement, and processing of student admission and progression documents. * Coordinates the intake of scholarship, grant, and internship applications. * Collects, collates, and analyzes student, recruitment, and enrollment data (including student service surveys, inquiry logs, participation tracking, and information requests) to prepare actionable reports and recommendations for the OSS Director. * Maintains competence in and utilizes current office technologies and tools including MS Office Suite, web-hosted services, shared drives, listservs, applicable University systems, and artificial intelligence (AI) applications to enhance administrative efficiency, productivity, data management, and communication in compliance with University security and privacy policies. * Interfaces with campus and system-wide student service departments to maintain effective working relationships and identify best practices in student support services. Keeps abreast of SONDH academic programs, admission requirements, recruitment initiatives, and University policies/procedures, as well as trends in student services, to effectively advise prospective students and inform the OSS Director and Admission/Student Affairs committees. May supervise student assistant(s). Performs other duties as assigned. (*) Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in business, education, communications, marketing, student services, or related field and two (2) years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for student services, student recruitment, enrollment outreach, admissions, communications, and/or office management in an academic environment; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills, and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices, and techniques in the area of student academic services, student recruitment, admissions, advising, or counseling, demonstrated by knowledge, understanding, and ability to apply concepts and terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations, and systems associated with higher education. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes, and resolve the full range of problems that may commonly occur in the areas of student services, student recruitment, enrollment outreach, and/or office management in an academic environment. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members, prospective students, current students, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing, presentation, spreadsheet, and other applicable software. Self-starter with demonstrated ability to learn new operating systems quickly. Demonstrated ability to be well-organized, multi-task, pay attention to detail, and effectively coordinate projects. Demonstrated ability to exercise good judgment, professionalism, problem-solving, and communication skills with a high standard of customer service when responding to complex and demanding situations, inquiries, or complaints. Demonstrated experience developing, coordinating, or supporting recruitment and outreach activities. Able to work outside of normal business hours, including evenings, weekends, and/or holidays, as required for student services, recruitment events, informational sessions, and outreach activities. Desirable Qualifications: Experience in a health professions school in higher education. Experience in student recruitment, enrollment outreach, admissions, community outreach, event coordination, marketing, communications, or related activities in a higher education environment. Experience communicating with prospective students, families, community organizations, campus partners, and other external constituents. Working knowledge of UH policies, procedures, and student information systems such as STAR and BANNER. Knowledge and understanding of FERPA regulations. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Resume; Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three (3) professional references; and Copies of unofficial transcripts (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries and copy of diplomas are NOT ACCEPTABLE. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. NOTE: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. Please do not include any self-identifying photos they will be redacted. PLEASE REDACT Social Security #s and birth dates on transcript copies. All submitted items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. No late applications will be accepted. Inquiries: Corie O. Fulgencio-Arre, HR Specialist; mcfa@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or adpaper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act(ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5542449/it-specialist-0081769": {
      "title": "IT Specialist (0081769)",
      "description": "IT Specialist (0081769) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02365 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Office of Campus Services Division Department of Public Safety Opening Date 11/05/2026 Closing Date 11/23/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0081769 Description Title: IT Specialist Position Number: 0081769 Hiring Unit: ADMINISTRATION, AUXIL ENT, AUXIL ENT Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: 11/05/2026 Closing Date: 11/23/2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities *Denotes Essential Functions *Oversees and actively participates throughout project life cycle, from understanding requirements to overseeing deployment and operational phases. *Assess, designs, develops, and implements solutions to meet business requirements and adhere to industry best practices for application and database services and support. *Evaluates project constraints to identify alternatives, mitigate risks, and re-engineer processes. *Creates, maintains, and enhances procedures, processes, and designs for software and systems. *Identifies and evaluates integration considerations and determine the impact to other systems at the University. Recommend a solution based on best practices; provide alternatives as needed. *Develops detailed project plans, including scope, objectives, testing scenarios, deliverables, and resource requirements. *Works with business end-users and/or project stakeholders to gather and document project's business requirements with consideration for interpretation, compliance, and application of state/federal laws, rules, regulations & Collective Bargaining Agreements. *Develops high-level business and user requirements into functional requirements that is documented in an appropriate level of detail and in a format suitable for the project team to manage. *Leads requirements analysis and verification, ensuring that requirement statements are complete, concise, comprehensible, traceable, feasible, verifiable, and conforms to established standards for new system implementations and/or upgrades to existing systems that introduces changes in features and functionalities. *Serves as a liaison between business end-users and technical teams to ensure high-level business goals align with technical system designs and data workflows. *Leads requirements validation activities to confirm that proposed solutions, based on the requirements, will meet organizational needs and achieve objectives. *Partners with University stakeholders, including IT teams, department heads, and external vendors to gather requirements, define technical specifications, and ensure system solutions meet administrative needs. *Coordinates end-user acceptance testing and validation. Participate and administer user acceptance testing and follow-up on issue resolution. *Assists in the development, implementation, and improvements to information systems and processes. *Identifies potential risks and obstacles to project success, proactively developing mitigation strategies, and adopts plans as needed. *Manages adherence to project scope; escalate as needed if stakeholders are deviating from scope so team can conduct feasibility assessment. *Troubleshoots issues and implements solutions to maintain project momentum. *Ensures compliance with ITS Information Security Office's policies & implements its initiatives including managing data governance requirements. Works with teams to evaluate and establish business transformation streamlining and processes leveraging technology and in accordance with best practices. Works with ITS resources and/or vendors to resolve any issues with the desktop applications, business applications, database or server. Collaborates with colleagues throughout the UH system on strategies, emerging technologies, best practices and ideas to continually improve services and promote use of technology. *Follows and implements directives and guidance related to best practices from University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Services. *Ensures the consistent adoption, implementation, and enforcement of recommendations issued through University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Service. *Keeps abreast of recommendations issued through University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Service, and takes timely action as needed. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a pertinent baccalaureate educational degree in Management Information Systems or Business Administration, or related field and 3 years of progressively responsible professional information technology experience with responsibilities for project management, application support, system administration and/or system integration, of which 2 years of the experience must have been comparable in scope and complexity to the next lower payband in the University of Hawai'i broadband system; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of project management, application support, system administration, and/or system integration as demonstrated by the broad knowledge and understanding of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving information technology concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of the broad technology, systems, hardware and software associated with applications, interfaces, databases, and network/infrastructure considerations. Demonstrated ability to recognize a wide range of intricate problems, use reasoning and logic to determine accurate causes, and apply principles and practices to determine, evaluate, integrate, and implement practical and thorough solutions in an effective and timely manner. Proven ability to comprehend, interpret and implement administrative directives and guidance to ensure IT operations align with organizational standards and industry best practices. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Strong understanding of IT service management, cybersecurity principles, risk management, and compliance requirements. Demonstrated experience implementing and maintaining IT best practices, standards, and governance. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external stakeholders to accomplish tasks in a timely and positive manner. Ability to meet deadlines and work outside normally scheduled work hours as needed to include day, night, weekend and/or holiday hours as directed. Desirable Qualifications Working knowledge of project management Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) methodologies. Knowledge of infrastructure, network and server requirements in support of applications. Project Management Professional (PMP) Certified. Strong knowledge of integration frameworks and methodologies. Experience in analyzing and documenting complex data flows and system end points. Experience with report writing tools and/or generating queries for reporting and data extracts. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Click on the \"Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents by the closing date: 1. Cover letter indicating how you meet the minimum and desirable qualifications; 2. Resume; 3. Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three (3) professional references (preferably supervisor); and 4. Copy of your official transcript showing highest degree. (Official transcripts will be required if selected to fill the position.) Note: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. Please do not include any self-identifying photos, and PLEASE REDACT REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBERS AND BIRTH DATE ON TRANSCRIPT COPIES. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii Office of Campus Services, and will not be returned. Inquiries: Campus Services Human Resources Office; 808-956-6935; uhmcshr@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5499807/research-associate-patient-navigator-0097716t-readvertisement": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Patient Navigator) - (0097716T) - READVERTISEMENT",
      "description": "Research Associate (Patient Navigator) - (0097716T) - READVERTISEMENT Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa - UH Cancer Center, Kaka'ako - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02071 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Opening Date 11/05/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 00997716T Description Benefits Description Title: Research Associate (Patient Navigator) – (0097716T) - READVERTIEMENT Position Number: 0097716T Hiring Unit: RES & DEAN OF GRAD DIV, CANCER CT HI, CANCER CT HI Location: Honolulu, Kaka'ako, Hawai'i Date Posted: November 5, 2026 Closing Date: November 16, 2026 at 11:59 P.M. Band : B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBB, minimum $5, 640/month Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities: *Provides patient navigation and cancer care access support to patients, families, caregivers, and healthcare providers for the Susan C. Hirano Cancer C.A.R.E. Community and the Ka Umeke Lama Medical Oncology Fellowship Program at the University of Hawaii Cancer Center. *Provides administrative, operational, and programmatic support to the planning, coordination, and implementation of activities associated with the Susan C. Hirano Cancer C.A.R.E. Community and the Ka Umeke Lama Medical Oncology Fellowship Program. *Serves as the primary point of contact for individuals seeking information about cancer diagnosis, treatment options, second opinions, clinical trials, supportive care services, and community resources. Serves as a trusted resource for patients and families navigating complex healthcare systems and seeking access to cancer-related services. *Conducts intake assessments to identify patient and family needs, barriers to care, and resource gaps. Provides information and guidance regarding available cancer treatment providers, healthcare facilities, specialty services, clinical trial opportunities, supportive care programs, transportation assistance, financial resources, survivorship services, and other relevant support systems. *Maintains current knowledge of local, national, and international cancer care resources, clinical trial databases, and referral networks. Assists individuals in understanding how to access clinical trial information and facilitates connections with appropriate healthcare providers and research institutions when requested. *Coordinates referrals and follow-up communication with community organizations, healthcare systems, patient advocacy groups, and research partners. Documents interactions and resource referrals in accordance with program policies and reporting requirements. *Provides educational materials and information while ensuring that all communications remain within the scope of patient navigation and do not constitute medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment recommendations. *Assists with organizing community events, educational workshops, support programs, wellness activities, and other community engagement initiatives. Coordinates event logistics, participant registration, communications, scheduling, and follow-up activities. *Maintains program records, participant databases, attendance tracking, and program evaluation data. Assists with preparing reports, collecting participant feedback, and documenting program outcomes to support continuous improvement and donor stewardship efforts. *Collaborates with Cancer Center faculty, staff, community partners, patient advocates, and volunteers to enhance program offerings and strengthen community engagement. *Provides administrative support for fellowship-related events, recruitment activities, reporting requirements, and other programmatic needs as the fellowship program develops and launches. *Collaborates with Ka Umeke Lama leadership and program staff to support efforts aimed at strengthening the oncology workforce and expanding access to cancer care throughout Hawaii and the Pacific region. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Nursing, Science or health-related field and three (3) years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for patient navigation, public health, clinical research, oncology support or equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of methods and procedures in the area of healthcare systems methods and procedures, oncology care delivery, patient navigation principles, community-based resources, and health equity issues affecting access to cancer care Considerable working knowledge and understanding of Federal and State laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with governing of human subjects research and familiarity with clinical trial operations and regulatory requirements. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Supplemental Qualifications: Familiarity with medical terminology and the challenges faced by individuals navigating cancer diagnosis and treatment is desirable. Specialized knowledge of specific cancer care sites conducting clinical trials and/or of areas such as education/training, regulatory, investigational drug accountability/storage, specimen collection/processing, database maintenance/reporting and auditing. Demonstrated knowledge in clinical trials, oncology research, and healthcare systems. Demonstrated ability to interact positively with clinical study participants and others involved with the research environment. Desirable Qualifications: Master's Degree from an accredited college or university in a health-related field, such as Nursing, Medical Records, Medical Technology or Pharmacology. Working knowledge of the University of Hawai`i (UH), the RCUH, and UHCC policies and procedures. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen and attach the required documents. Applicants must submit 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2) resume, 3) names and contact information of three (3) professional references and 4) official transcripts (OR original documentation from the institution confirming degree OR copy of U.S. board certification). Copy of transcript is acceptable in place of U.S. board certification for evaluation purposes but original is required upon hire. Transcripts issued outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA will be required before date of hire. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant (Note: Only complete applications will be considered.) NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: hr@cc.hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Address 701 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
