ram staff across the U.S. Affiliated Pacific Islands (USAPI) and Hawai‘i supporting forest and land stewardship, agroforestry, wildfire prevention, and community-based resource management and resilience. This team works in partnership with the College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), the U.S. Forest Service (USFS) Pacific Southwest Region (R5), the Southwest Climate Hub (SWCH), land-grant colleges and universities across the USAPI, and State and territorial forestry programs (including agricultural and natural resources divisions where appropriate). While each position is based in and responsive to local needs, the Agents are expected to share knowledge, coordinate activities, and contribute to regional programming and activities through joint training, peer-to-peer learning, and collaborative projects. Together, this network of Junior Extension Agents supports the development and implementation of Forest Landowner Support Plans that align program delivery across project partners to meet the needs of local stakeholders at all scales, including but not limited to small-acreage forest landowners and stewards. Duties and Responsibilities: Under general supervision with some latitude for independent judgment, carries out the details of the Extension Program prescribed in the Annual Program of Work to build capacity for forestry, agroforestry, resource management, and forest resilience within the Republic of Palau. Extension programming may include work at the scale of individual landowners, communities, agencies/organizations locally, and/or multiple Pacific Island territories or Freely-Associated States (FAS), depending on interests, needs, and priorities of local and regional stakeholders. The Junior Extension Agent, with support from experts at regional land-grant institutions,regional forestry offices, CTAHR, USFS, and SWCH may contribute to Program Areas as needed and requested by program staff, program partners, landowners, and land stewards. 