ions, forest landowners and stewards, community-based initiatives, and other regional stakeholders. *Collaborate with CTAHR, USFS, and local project partners to develop and implement a local Forest Landowner Support Plan that aligns with the regional framework and addresses needs at all scales, including those of small-acreage forest landowners and stewards. *Work closely with the USFS, SWCH, and CTAHR to ensure program integration with regional resilience strategies. *Develop and deliver extension materials (factsheets, training modules, in-field workshops, presentations, etc.), resources, and content in-person and/or remotely with local language translation available, as requested. Capacity Building and Network Development *Build and maintain positive working relationships with program partners, PCC-CRE, the MAFE, community associations, other local organizations engaged in relevant work, project beneficiaries, and other regional stakeholders *Engage in cross-jurisdiction collaboration with PFRP staff, subject-matter experts, and extension agents in other USAPI and Hawai'i locations. *Facilitate information exchange through workshops, community meetings, and peer-to-peer learning networks to share best practices, provide guidance on adaptive management, and integrate current and emerging research into extension program delivery. *Participate in and/or lead meetings, as necessary, with PFRP staff, project partners, stakeholders, and other extension personnel to share progress, facilitate knowledge exchange, and co-develop regional strategies. *Collaborate with CTAHR, USFS, and project partners to seek and apply for internal and external grant funding to support extension program objectives. Program Support *Participate in workshops and meetings, as-needed, in Hawai'i and USAPI locations to support program goals *Participate in meetings, as necessary, with PRFP staff to evaluate progress in extension program delivery and align project activities with emerging program needs or objectives *Document, track, and evaluate program activities, outputs, and outcomes for both internal program improvement (including in PEARS) and external reporting to funding agencies and partners. *Identify new stakeholder interests and issues and work with program partners to share success stories through narratives, podcasts, videos, or other media. *Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree from a college or university of recognized standing, with major work in agriculture, forestry, natural resources management, environmental science, or a related field. Evidence of ability to conduct extension and outreach programming in agriculture, forestry, natural resources management, environmental science, or a related field. Professionalism in meeting and conferring with others. Demonstrated proficiency in MS Office (Word, Excel, PowerPoint). Excellent written and verbal communication, and technical writing skills Must possess a valid driver’s license, or the ability to obtain one. Desirable Qualifications Master of Science degree from a college or university of recognized standing, with major work in agriculture, forestry, natural resources management, environmental science, or a related field. Experience working in the Republic of Palau, and/or tropical or subtropical island forestry, agroforestry, or natural resources management. Proficiency in a Palauan language and/or another Pacific Island language Knowledge of Pacific Island plant species, nursery operations, and forest health monitoring. Familiarity with forestry and agroforestry stewardship practices, hazard prevention, and human health and nutrition. Experience conducting outreach, workshops, and field demonstrations for landowners, community groups, or agency staff. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with local agencies, educational institutions, and community organizations. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare educational materials and technical reports. Ability to author grant applications and manage budgets. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter (1 to 2 pages) indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Statement of extension experience (1 to 2 pages) and interests related to forestry, agroforestry, land stewardship, community resilience, and/or other issues, priorities, or strategies identified in the Republic of Palau Forest Action Plan (2021–2030). Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Ashley McGuigan; 808-969-8217; aamcguig@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ 01 Do you have a bachelor’s degree from a college or university of recognized standing, with major work in agriculture, forestry, natural resources management, environmental science, or a related field? Yes No 02 I confirm that I have attached a statement of extension experience and interests related to forestry, agroforestry, land stewardship, community resilience, and/or other issues, priorities, or strategies identified in the Forest Action Plan for this jurisdiction. Yes No 03 Complete applications and all requested document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you attest that you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
