    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531107/administrative-officer-0097754t": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer (0097754T)",
      "description": "Administrative Officer (0097754T) Location UH Manoa - HIMB, Coconut Island - Kaneohe, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02297 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - SOEST - Hawai'i Institute of Marine Biology Opening Date 11/09/2026 Closing Date 11/30/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097754T Description Title: Administrative Officer Position Number: 0097754T Hiring Unit: RES & DEAN OF GRAD DIV, SCH O&ES&T, HAW INST OF MAR BIO Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: 11/09/26 Closing Date: 11/30/26 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Continuation subject to availability of funds, satisfactory performance, and program needs. Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Coordinates day-to-day pre-award and post-award grants administration for assigned Principal Investigators and extramural projects. 2. *Guides and assist faculty and staff in budget development and document submission through myGRANT ensuring that proposed budget conform to applicable rules, regulations, policies, and procedures. 3. *Reviews all grant proposals, budgets and justifications to ensure compliance with funding and oversight agency rules and regulations; approves if appropriate. 4. *Monitors routine project financial operations, expenditure controls, and baseline budget tracking for assigned extramural grants. 5. *Reviews and approves procurement, personnel, travel, and disbursement transactions for allowability, allocability, reasonableness, and availability of funds using UH Systems (example: KFS and RCUH) 6. *Monitors account end dates, unexpended balances and encumbrances, coordinates account extensions, closeouts, and corrective entries. 7. *Applies federal, state, university, and sponsored agreement terms and guidelines to daily financial operations. 8. *Exercises fiscal signing authority up to $10,000 for standard operational purchases. 9. *Provides accurate and timely monthly budget projection reports of account status to assigned principal investigators 10. *Prepares budget and expenditure statement for principal investigator regularly and reviews monthly expenditure reports to more efficiently administer the grant and avoid potential deficit situations. 11. *Monitors account end dates, and account balances. Works with assigned principal investigator to take appropriate action to terminate or extend accounts. 12. *Reviews and approves personnel appointment forms ensuring compliance with granting guidelines as well as University policies 13. *Ensures proper recording of expenditures and encumbrances. Initiates corrective action for erroneous entries. 14. *Researches and applies standard terms and conditions of sponsored agreements to ensure compliance. 15. *Serves as a point of contact for PIs and staff regarding standard administrative, procurement, and fiscal procedures. 16. *Prepares non-recurring reports, summaries, and routine correspondence for internal review. 17. *Recommends operational improvements to internal procedures to improve efficiency. 18. *Participates on special committees or workgroups to assist with unit financial planning and reporting. 19. Performs other duties as assigned. *Denotes essential functions Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business, Accounting, Finance or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for fiscal and/or contracts/grants administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of fiscal and/or contracts/grants administration as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with fiscal and/or contracts/grants administration. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Demonstrated ability to analyze, interpret, & use sound logic & judgment when applying administrative policies and procedures. 10. Demonstrated ability to prioritize and organize workload to meet scheduled deadlines. 11. Knowledge of accepted governmental accounting principles, techniques and fund accounting systems. 12. Knowledge of the principles and practices of federal and state fund administration. 13. A bility to analyze financial statements and statistical reports, discern trends, and apply analytical techniques. 14. Familiarity with spreadsheet and word processing applications such as Microsoft Excel and Word. Desirable Qualifications 1. Familiarity with the UH and RCUH accounting, procurement, and personnel systems. 2. Knowledge of administrative practices of the University and/or other institutions of higher education. To Apply : Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen and attach the required documents. Applicants must submit the following: (1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; (2) resume; (3) names and contact information of 3 professional references; and (4) copies of transcripts with confidential identifiable information (i.e. social security number, birth date, etc.) redacted are acceptable, but official transcripts will be required at the time of hire. Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Diplomas will not be accepted. All requested documents/information will become the property of the University. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries: Edwin Payuan; 808-236-7404; epayuan@hawaii.edu Please refer to \"Admin Officer Pos 0097754T\" when making inquiries. EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - SOEST - Hawai'i Institute of Marine Biology Address 46-007 Lilipuna Rd Kaneohe, Hawaii, 96744 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5545478/athletics-compliance-specialist-0081890": {
      "title": "Athletics Compliance Specialist (0081890)",
      "description": "Athletics Compliance Specialist (0081890) Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02393 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 11/09/2026 Closing Date 11/24/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0081890 Description Title: Athletics Compliance Specialist Position Number: 0081890 Hiring Unit: INT ATHLETICS UHM, INT ATHLETICS UHM, INT ATHLETICS UHM Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: November 9, 2026 Closing Date: November 24, 2026 Band: A or B Salary: salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions : Filling one (1) position only. Pay Band A - Duties and Responsibilities *Under the direction of the Assistant Athletics Director for Compliance and Eligibility, the incumbent provides NCAA compliance support to the Athletics Compliance Department. *Assists in evaluating and maintaining accurate student-athlete eligibility and participation records. *Assists with updating and maintaining Recruiting and Compliance Systems (ARMS) and monitor the recordkeeping of recruiting activities of coaching staff (e.g. contact and evaluation logs, phone logs and countable activity logs) ensuring compliance with NCAA rules and regulations. Assists with NCAA Bylaw 17, Playing and Practice Seasons, including but not limited to countable athletically related activities (CARA), time management plan (TMP), scheduling and Sports Sponsorship. *Assists with distribution of educational information/materials to coaches, student-athletes, boosters and the general community on NCAA rules and regulations. *Assists with the Department's drug education and testing program in a professional and accurate manner (i.e. schedule appointments, record and file laboratory and University forms). *Assists with data entry of reports for the Department, Institution, affiliated Conferences and the NCAA as requested. *Assists in updating and maintaining all NCAA squad list documents in the NCAA Compliance Assistant internet software. *Assists in working with the Athletics' Webmaster with managing the Compliance section of the UHM Athletics website and ensuring that all information are accurate and current. *Responds to inquiries related to compliance. Consult with Assistant Athletic Director of Compliance and Eligibility for complex matters. *Coordinates ordering and disseminating of compliance forms and manuals to coaches and staff. *Assists with managing ARMS software processes. Assist with creating, maintaining and updating all workflows in ARMS. Review data for accuracy, review processes, troubleshoot and identify areas of improvement. *Assists with coordinating and facilitate training session for employees and student-athletes on the use of ARMS. Ensures that users are aware of the importance of accurate and timely data entry and compliance. *Reviews and identify policies and procedures that may be incorporated in ARMS and may propose policy/procedures updates and/or changes to the Assistant Athletics Director of Compliance/Eligibility. *Assists Assistant Athletics Director of Compliance/Eligibility with updating forms and documents to ensure compliance for NCAA rules and regulations. *Assists with planning efficient ways to develop ARMS software to better serve the UHMAD (e.g. data collection, expedite processing of forms, distribution of policies/procedures, camp proposals, etc.) while ensuring compliance with UH policies and procedures, Conference and NCAA rules and regulations. *Assists with handling issues related to ARMS, identify the problem, and formulate a plan on resolving the issues. Implement preventive and proactive measures to minimize future issues and challenges with ARMS. *Assists in the initial eligibility certification process and in the transcript evaluation process for prospective incoming freshmen and transfer student-athletes. *Assists with various compliance related activities that may occur during nights, weekends and holidays to meet programmatic needs. Responsible for performing the duties of a Campus Security Authority as defined by University policy and federal law with regards to the Clery Act. Maintains a collaborative and positive working relationships with administrators, coaches, student-athletes, boosters and other college offices, and appropriate relationships with other individuals interested in the Athletics Department. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Knows, recognizes, and complies with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if there is reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees, students, or others have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Functions Pay Band A – Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in arts and sciences, business administration, education or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for NCAA compliance administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of intercollegiate athletics demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules regulations and systems associated with intercollegiate athletics. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of intercollegiate athletics. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software and spreadsheet processing software. Some experience in NCAA intercollegiate athletics. Some knowledge of NCAA rules and regulations. Ability to work effectively under pressure, with frequent interruptions, and remain flexible in handling several concurrent projects with competing deadlines. Demonstrated ability to exercise discretion and sound analyses to a variety of administrative support, program management or related issues. Ability to handle confidential information with a high level of tact, discretion, and diplomacy. Demonstrated ability to pay attention to details. Ability to work a flexible schedule depending on programmatic needs. Pay Band A - Desirable Qualifications Master's degree in Sports Management, Juris Doctorate degree or related field. Experience with NCAA intercollegiate athletics compliance. Experience in ARMS software. Demonstrated commitment to valuing diversity, understanding and working effectively in multicultural societies, similar to Hawaii, contributing to an inclusive environment. Pay Band B - Duties and Responsibilities *Under the direction of the Assistant Athletics Director for Compliance and Eligibility, the incumbent provides NCAA compliance support to the Athletics Compliance Department. *Monitors, updates and maintains the Recruiting and Compliance System consisting of recruiting activities of coaching staff. *Reviews, analyzes and ensures recruiting activities are in compliance with NCAA rules/regulations and proper practices are implemented/enforced. *Identifies, investigates and resolves and/or recommends resolutions for any NCAA violation or potential NCAA violations. *Advises and educates coaches, student-athletes, boosters and the general community on NCAA rules and regulations. *Maintains current records regarding interpretation and procedures of affiliated conferences and NCAA. Makes necessary changes to record keeping practices and documentation of athletic activities to ensure continued compliance. *Tracks NCAA and affiliated Conference legislative changes and interpretations of the rules and performs analysis of the application of rules as intended. Communicates pertinent changes to the department of new and/or upcoming changes in institutional NCAA and affiliated conference rules and regulations. *Provides rules clarifications and interpretations to department staff as requested. Develops and organizes a system for dissemination and documentation of information to further institutional control. *Responsible for all aspects of NCAA Bylaw 17, Playing and Practice Seasons, including but not limited to countable athletically related activities (CARA), time management plan (TMP), scheduling and Sports Sponsorship. Interprets NCAA Bylaw 17 to coaches, advises and independently answers inquiries and resolves issues to ensure compliance. *Reviews for compliance the Athletic Aid Agreement documents received from student-athletes and/or coaches and uploads relevant information in the Athletic Aid Agreement portal. *Coordinates with the Assistant Athletics Director for Compliance and Eligibility the department's drug education and testing program for student-athletes in a professional and accurate manner (i.e. schedule appointments, record and file laboratory and University forms). Must be able to work a flexible schedule depending on programmatic needs. *Responsible for updating and maintaining all NCAA squad list documents in the NCAA Compliance Assistant internet software. *Updates and maintains all eligibility spreadsheets; responsible for obtaining all eligibility tracers, transfer credits and other transfer eligibility as needed. *Responsible for writing and submitting waivers to NCAA for Amateurism purposes. *Prepares reports for the Department, Institution, affiliated Conferences and the NCAA (e.g. progress toward degree reports, initial eligibility waiver reports, Equity in Athletics Disclosure Act reports). Recommends to Assistant Athletics Director new training programs, changes in departmental procedures or documentation of activities to ensure compliance with the most current NCAA rules and regulations. *Manages ARMS software processes. Creates, maintains and updates all workflows in ARMS. Reviews data for accuracy, reviews processes, troubleshoots and identifies areas of improvement. Coordinates and facilitates training sessions for employees and student-athletes on the use of ARMS. Ensures that users are aware of the importance of accurate and timely data entry and compliance. *Reviews and identifies all updates to policies and procedures are up to date in ARMS and proposes policy and procedure updates and/or changes to the Assistant Athletics Director of Compliance/Eligibility. *Responsible for handling issues related to ARMS, identifying the problem, and formulating a plan on resolving the issues. *Responsible for communication with ARMS software company. Serves as main point of contact for all ARMS issues including workflow and system issues. Works with the Athletics' Webmaster with managing the Compliance section of the UHM Athletics website and ensuring that all information are accurate and up-to-date. Updates forms and documents to ensure compliance for NCAA rules and regulations under the direction of the Assistant Athletics Director. *Assist with managing the student-athlete guest tickets gate for all sports with admission. May include work at nights, weekends and/or holidays. *Assists in the initial eligibility certification process and in the transcript evaluation process for prospective incoming freshmen and transfer student-athletes. Responsible for performing the duties of a Campus Security Authority as defined by University policy and federal law with regards to the Clery Act. Maintains a collaborative and positive working relationships with administrators, coaches, student-athletes, boosters and other college offices, and appropriate relationships with other individuals interested in the Athletics Department. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Knows, recognizes, and complies with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if there is a reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees, students, or others have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Functions Pay Band B - Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in arts and sciences, humanities, social sciences, natural sciences, business administration, education or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for NCAA compliance administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the field of NCAA Compliance as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with intercollegiate athletics. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software, and spreadsheet processing software. For supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, manage employee relations. Some experience with NCAA Division I compliance administration. Knowledge of NCAA rules and regulations in the areas of eligibility, amateurism, financial aid and recruiting, playing and practice seasons. Demonstrated ability to work effectively under pressure, with frequent interruptions, and remain flexible in handling several concurrent projects with competing deadlines. Demonstrated ability to handle sensitive and confidential information. Demonstrated ability to exercise discretion and sound analyses to a variety of administrative support, program management or related issues. Demonstrated ability to handle confidential information with a high level of tact, discretion, and diplomacy. Demonstrated ability to pay attention to details and strong organizational skills. Demonstrated ability to effectively communicate with coaches, administrators, student-athletes, parents, faculty, staff, and high school staff members. Demonstrated ability to educate and interact with staff, coaches, student-athletes, university community and the general public. Ability to work a flexible schedule depending on programmatic needs. Work includes weekends, evenings, holidays, and the requirements to attend sporting events. Pay Band B – Desirable Qualifications Master's degree in Sports Management, Juris Doctorate degree or related field. Experience in ARMS software. Demonstrated commitment to valuing diversity, understanding and working effectively in multicultural societies, similar to Hawai`i, contributing to an inclusive environment. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating which position(s) you are interested in and how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, and 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number). Inquiries: Laura Beeman, Associate Athletics Director for Student-Athlete Success, lbeeman@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5545172/graduate-research-assistant-0000ga11-ha-kupuna-natl-res-ctr-for-native-hawaiian": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11, Ha Kupuna Natl Res Ctr for Native Hawaiian Elders)",
      "description": "Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11, Ha Kupuna Natl Res Ctr for Native Hawaiian Elders) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02390 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Thompson School of Social Work & Public Health Opening Date 11/09/2026 Closing Date 11/23/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0000GA11 Description Title: Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11, Ha Kupuna National Resource Center for Native Hawaiian Elders) Position Number: 0000GA11 Hiring Unit: Thompson School of Social Work & Public Health Location: University of Hawai'i at Manoa Date Posted: November 9, 2026 Closing Date: November 23, 2026 Salary: Minimum salary: $2,335.50 per month. Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 11-month Full Time/Part Time: Part Time (.50 FTE) Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Funding: Federal funds Other Conditions Appointment to begin approximately January 2027 or as soon thereafter as possible. Continuation is dependent upon satisfactory performance and availability of funding. Some flexibility in work schedule is available. Occasional travel to community sites may be required. Position Description Ha Kupuna National Resource Center for Native Hawaiian Elders seeks a Graduate Research Assistant interested in Indigenous health, aging, and community-engaged research. Funded by the Administration for Community Living (ACL), Ha Kupuna works with Native Hawaiian elders, caregivers, community organizations, and service providers to strengthen services and promote healthy aging. During the 2026–2029 grant cycle, Ha Kupuna will focus on three areas: (1) connecting Native Hawaiian elders to services, (2) promoting whole-person health, and (3) supporting family and formal caregivers. Activities include community-based research, development and evaluation of culturally relevant educational resources, and dissemination of findings to strengthen services for Native Hawaiian kupuna. The Graduate Research Assistant will work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and community partners to support research and program activities. This position offers opportunities to develop skills in community-engaged research, qualitative methods, program evaluation, and knowledge dissemination. Duties and Responsibilities Assist with community-engaged research involving Native Hawaiian kupuna, family caregivers, service providers, and community organizations, including recruitment, scheduling, focus groups, and interviews. Support qualitative research activities, including transcription review, data organization, coding, thematic analysis, and preparation of research summaries. Assist with the development, cultural adaptation, testing, and evaluation of educational materials and interventions related to healthy aging, brain health, fall prevention, medication management, advance care planning, caregiving, and intergenerational knowledge sharing. Support program evaluation activities, including developing data collection tools, administering surveys, organizing evaluation data, and documenting participant feedback and program outcomes. Conduct literature reviews and assist in reviewing and synthesizing research related to Indigenous aging, Native Hawaiian health, culturally grounded interventions, and caregiving. Assist in preparing research findings and educational resources for dissemination through reports, manuscripts, conference presentations, fact sheets, and other materials for academic and community audiences. Collaborate with project staff and community partners to support workshops, training activities, and community events. Participate in regular project meetings, maintain research records, and assist with tracking activities and deliverables. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Classified, degree-seeking graduate student enrolled in a master's or doctoral program at the University of Hawai'i at Manoa in social work, public health, Hawaiian studies, psychology, sociology, or a related field. Good academic standing and compliance with University of Hawai'i Graduate Division requirements for graduate assistant appointments. Demonstrated interest in Indigenous health, Native Hawaiian communities, aging, gerontology, or caregiving. Interest in community-engaged research and working collaboratively with community partners. Strong written and oral communication skills. Strong interpersonal skills and demonstrated ability to work respectfully with individuals from diverse cultural and professional backgrounds. Ability to work independently, manage multiple tasks, and collaborate effectively as part of a research team. Proficiency with common computer applications, including word processing, spreadsheets, and presentation software. Availability to participate in regularly scheduled project meetings and community research activities. Desirable Qualifications Doctoral standing or prior graduate-level research experience. Experience with or coursework in Indigenous research methodologies, community-based participatory research, or culturally grounded research approaches. Experience working with Native Hawaiian communities, Indigenous communities, older adults, or family caregivers. Experience with qualitative research methods, including interviews, focus groups, transcription, coding, or thematic analysis. Experience with program development, implementation, or evaluation. Familiarity with qualitative data analysis software (e.g., NVivo) or quantitative data management and analysis. Knowledge of Native Hawaiian culture, history, language, and community contexts. Experience developing research reports, manuscripts, educational resources, or community-facing dissemination materials. Knowledge of aging-related issues, including dementia, healthy aging, long-term care, caregiving, and access to health and social services. To Apply: Click on the \"APPLY\" button on the top right-hand corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the following REQUIRED documents/information. 1. Current resume or curriculum vitae; 2. A cover letter describing how you meet the minimum and desirable qualifications; 3. Names and contact information (including email addresses) of at least 3 professional references; 4. Official transcripts or equivalent documents from the institution confirming the highest educational degree relevant to this position (copies acceptable, official originals required upon hire). Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Miquela Ibrao at 808-956-4494; email: mibrao@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5545071/operations-coordinator-0078055": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator (0078055)",
      "description": "Operations Coordinator (0078055) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02389 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Office of the Provost Opening Date 11/09/2026 Closing Date 11/24/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0078055 Description Title: Operations Coordinator Position Number: 0078055 Hiring Unit: OFFICE OF THE PROVOST, OFFICE OF THE PROVOST, OFFICE OF THE PROVOST Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: November 9, 2026 Closing Date: November 24, 2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Reports to the Senior Administrative Assistant to the Provost and serves as an administrative resource, providing comprehensive administrative and operational support to the Office of the Provost and Provost leadership team as needed. Administers executive-level coordination, office operations, scheduling, communications, academic program initiatives, and special projects to support the effective functioning of the Provost's Office. 2. *Oversees the progression of high-priority campus requests, strategic initiatives, academic program initiatives, and institutional directives from assignment to completion; initiates and enforces follow-up actions across campus units to ensure executive deadlines are met. 3. *Researches, analyzes, synthesizes, and maintains integrated tracking systems; organizes information to prepare special, nonrecurring written reports, briefing summaries, and supporting materials; and develops project tracking logs for senior leadership related to office operations, academic programs, and ongoing campus initiatives. 4. *Evaluates daily office procedures, including document management workflows, routing systems for incoming correspondence, records management, and executive meeting protocols. Develops improved administrative procedures to enhance office efficiency and effectiveness. 5. *Provides administrative and operational support to the Senior Administrative Assistant to the Provost and Provost leadership team by coordinating project timelines, monitoring progress on assigned initiatives, identifying potential scheduling or administrative challenges, and recommending solutions to support the effective operation of the Office of the Provost and the timely completion of office priorities and academic program initiatives. 6. *Independently resolves routine operational and administrative matters by applying a thorough understanding of University policies, procedures, and executive directives, and responds to inquiries from internal and external stakeholders. 7. *Provides procedural information and administrative support to colleges, academic units, program offices, and campus personnel regarding Provost's Office processes, established academic program procedures, executive correspondence, and applicable service timelines. 8. *Coordinates logistics, scheduling, and operational details for executive meetings, system-wide campus visits, institutional events, and other high-level executive engagements supporting the Office of the Provost. 9. *Monitors and maintains institutional calendars, including Board of Regents, academic calendars, University deadlines, and other key academic and administrative dates, to support timely planning and coordination across the Provost's Office. 10. Maintains comprehensive tracking logs and records of key actions, decisions, deadlines, and ongoing matters to support institutional memory, continuity of operations, and effective transition of information within the Provost's Office. 11. Manages fiscal accountability and purchasing documentation for supported offices, including p-card transactions, purchase orders, and equipment and supply inventory, in accordance with University policies and regulations. 12. Supervises, trains, and schedules student assistants, setting clear performance expectations to maintain a professional, secure, and confidential environment. 13. Other duties as assigned. NOTE: * denotes Essential functions Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Communications, or a related field and 3 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for office management, operations coordination, or executive administrative support; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of office administration as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with office administration. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and proficiency using Microsoft Office applications (Word, Excel, Outlook, and PowerPoint) and Google Workspace applications (Docs, Sheets, Drive, Gmail, and Calendar). 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Demonstrated ability to handle highly confidential and sensitive information with discretion, integrity, and sound judgment. 10. Demonstrated ability to exercise independent judgment in determining the appropriate handling, sharing, retention, and safeguarding of confidential information. 11. Demonstrated ability to represent an executive office with professionalism, tact, diplomacy, and discretion in communications and interactions with internal and external constituents. Desirable Qualifications 1. Experience working in a higher education environment providing direct operational support within an executive or central administrative office. 2. Familiarity with the University of Hawaii system structure, campus operations, and applicable institutional administrative rules and policies. 3. Demonstrated ability to handle, process, and protect highly sensitive and confidential executive information with complete discretion. 4. Ability to work early mornings, nights, weekends and holidays, as needed, to support Provost's priorities and campus events. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Applicants must submit the following: 1. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; 2. Resume or CV ; 3. Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of 3 professional references; 4. Copy of unofficial transcript (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries will NOT be accepted. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted.) Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. IMPORTANT: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the Last one will be reviewed. Please DO NOT include any self-identifying photos, REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBERS AND BIRTH DATE on any documents within the application packet. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. Incomplete or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Inquiries: Robyn Okumura, 808-956-8447 chowhoyr@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5545647/readvertisement-marketing-specialist-0077481t": {
      "title": "READVERTISEMENT - Marketing Specialist (0077481T)",
      "description": "READVERTISEMENT - Marketing Specialist (0077481T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02398 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Press Opening Date 11/09/2026 Closing Date 11/30/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0077481T Description Title: Marketing Specialist Position Number: 0077481T Hiring Unit: UHM LIBRARY SERVICES, UH PRESS, MARKETING DEPARTMENT Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa, UH Press (2840 Kolowalu Street, Honolulu, HI 96822) Date Posted: 11/09/2026 Closing Date: 11/30/2026 Pay Band: B (PBB) Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions): *Plans and executes integrated digital marketing campaigns aligned with UH Press publishing, publicity, and sales strategies. *Develops, manages, and optimizes paid digital advertising campaigns (Google Ads, Meta, Amazon) to increase visibility and revenue. *Designs and implements targeted email marketing campaigns, including segmentation, A/B testing, and performance analysis. *Monitors and improves website search engine operations (SEO) performance through keyword research, metadata optimization, and analytics tools. *Maintains and updates accurate metadata across internal systems and external platforms (e.g., Amazon, website, distribution channels). *Analyzes campaign performance using analytics tools and provide actionable insights to improve strategy. Creates digital assets for marketing campaigns, including graphics, ads, and web content. Collaborates with internal teams to ensure consistent branding and messaging across platforms. Stays current on digital marketing trends, tools, and publishing industry best practices. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Education and Professional Work Experience: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in marketing,, communications, English, or related field and 3 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for marketing or communications; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of marketing or communications as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with marketing communications. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing and standard office software, and digital marketing software. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Supplemental Minimum Qualifications: Considerable working knowledge of digital marketing principles, search engine optimization (SEO), analytics, and advertising platforms. Demonstrated ability to analyze data and resolve complex marketing challenges. Desirable Qualifications: Experience using Biblio, Naviga, or a similar relational database, and working knowledge of using HTML and ONIX for managing publishing metadata. Proficiency with graphic design software such as Canva, Adobe InDesign, or equivalent tools. Experience in marketing books, journals, or other publications, particularly within academic or trade publishing. Familiarity with email marketing platforms such as ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp, Constant Contact, or Salesforce Marketing Cloud. Working knowledge of search engine optimization (SEO) best practices, including keyword research, metadata optimization, and analytics tools such as Google Analytics or Google Search Console. Experience managing and optimizing digital advertising campaigns on platforms such as Google Ads, Facebook/Meta Ads, or Amazon Ads. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents: (1) Cover letter that addresses how the applicant meets the minimum qualifications: degree requirements, experience, knowledge, skills, and abilities, as well as the desirable qualifications; (2) resume; (3) names and contact information (telephone numbers and email addresses) of at least three professional references; and, (4) official transcript(s) showing degree and coursework to date appropriate to the position (copies of official transcripts are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to completion of hiring). Late and/or incomplete (applications that do not include all four documents) will not be considered. All documents become the property of the school. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Nicole Su-Gannet, Screening Committee Chair, sugannet@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    }
